window.COURSE_CHUNK_en_home={"chunk":"home","lang":"en","pages":{
"course-home-about-the-course":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"Welcome"},
{"type":"para","text":["Welcome to ",{"t":"i","x":"Basics of Vedic Astrology: A Complete Beginner's Course"},", presented by ASTROSUTRAS by Anoopam Shukla. The course has fourteen modules and is self-paced: you study when you can and repeat whatever is unclear. It assumes no earlier knowledge of astrology. It asks only for curiosity, a notebook and the patience to work one step at a time."]},
{"type":"para","text":"The course has a single aim. It teaches you to read a horoscope in an orderly way. You will learn what a chart contains, why each rule exists, how to check a rule's conditions before you use it, and how to put a finding into careful words. A chart can look like a puzzle on the first day. Each module adds one more piece, and the course shows you how the pieces fit. The tagline sums it up in three sentences."},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understand the Principles. Analyse the Relationships. Build the Foundation."}]},
{"type":"para","text":["The promise of the course is modest and specific. When you finish, you will not be an expert predictive astrologer, and no short course can make you one. You will have a sound foundation, a method you can repeat on any chart, and a clear sense of what a chart can and cannot tell you. ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Level and Intended Audience","s":"course-home-course-level"}," explains this in detail."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"What Is Vedic Astrology?"},
{"type":"para","text":["Vedic astrology is the English name for ",{"t":"i","x":"Jyotisha"},", the traditional Indian discipline of reading the heavens. The Sanskrit word relates to light and to the bodies that give light. In tradition, Jyotisha is one of the six ",{"t":"i","x":"Vedangas"},", the \"limbs of the Veda\": supporting disciplines that serve Vedic ritual. Jyotisha is the limb concerned with timekeeping and with choosing suitable times for ritual. Today the word also names the wider tradition of casting and reading charts."]},
{"type":"para","text":"Three features mark the approach taught here."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"A star-based zodiac."}," Signs are measured against the fixed stars. This is the ",{"t":"i","x":"sidereal zodiac"},". A correction called the ",{"t":"i","x":"Ayanamsha"}," converts positions from the tropical zodiac, which is measured from the March equinox."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"A rising sign."}," The sign rising on the eastern horizon at birth anchors the chart and fixes the order of its houses."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Planetary periods."}," A timing system divides time into periods ruled by planets, counted from the Moon's position at birth."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":["There is no single rulebook in Jyotisha: texts, teachers and software differ on some definitions. This course follows the ",{"t":"i","x":"Parashari"}," approach, named after Parashara, the traditional author of the classical text Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra. It states its defaults openly and names the main variants in boxes marked SCHOOLS, so that you never mistake one convention for the only correct one."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"What a Kundali Represents"},
{"type":"para","text":["A ",{"t":"i","x":"Kundali"}," is a chart of the sky for one moment and one place, drawn as seen from the Earth. A ",{"t":"i","x":"Janma Kundali"}," is the Kundali made for a birth (",{"t":"i","x":"janma"}," means birth). In English it is called a horoscope or a birth chart. Picture the sky above the birthplace at the minute of birth. The Sun, the Moon and the planets stand at particular places along the zodiac, and one sign is just rising over the eastern horizon. The Kundali is a drawing of that moment."]},
{"type":"para","text":"Calculation gives three kinds of fact: which sign is rising, which sign holds each planet, and which house each sign becomes. These are positions, and they can be recalculated and checked. What a position suggests is a separate matter. That is interpretation, a judgement that depends on rules, conditions and the reader. This course keeps the two apart, and you will learn to do the same."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"Six Ideas You Will Meet Everywhere"},
{"type":"para","text":"Six ideas run through every module. Here is what each is and why it matters."},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h5","text":"The Lagna"},
{"type":"para","text":["The ",{"t":"i","x":"Lagna"},", or Ascendant, is the sign and degree rising on the eastern horizon at the moment of birth. It becomes the 1st house, and every other house is counted from it. It matters because it makes a chart personal. On average the rising sign changes about every two hours, so two people born in the same place on the same day can have different Lagnas. This is why the birth time matters. ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.4","s":"module-01-lesson-04-lagna-rashis-bhavas-and-grahas"}," introduces it, and Module 9 studies all twelve."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h6","text":"The Bhavas"},
{"type":"para","text":["The twelve ",{"t":"i","x":"Bhavas"},", or houses, divide a chart into areas of life, from the self in the 1st house through partnership in the 7th to career in the 10th. They matter because a planet is read in the area where it stands and in the areas it rules. No house is read alone. ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 2","s":"module-02-introduction"}," teaches them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h7","text":"The Rashis"},
{"type":"para","text":["The twelve ",{"t":"i","x":"Rashis"},", or signs, are 30-degree divisions of the zodiac, each with its own qualities and ruling planet. They matter because the sign shapes the manner in which a planet or a house expresses itself. Signs are fixed in the sky, while houses are counted from the Lagna, and the course keeps these two ideas apart. ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 4","s":"module-04-introduction"}," teaches them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h8","text":"The Grahas"},
{"type":"para","text":["The nine ",{"t":"i","x":"Grahas"}," are the Sun, the Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus and Saturn, together with Rahu and Ketu, two calculated points where the Moon's path crosses the Sun's path in the sky. Each is traditionally associated with certain themes, and its role in a chart is read from its sign, house, lordship and company. ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 3","s":"module-03-introduction"}," teaches them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h9","text":"The Nakshatras"},
{"type":"para","text":["The 27 ",{"t":"i","x":"Nakshatras"}," divide the zodiac into equal parts of 13°20' each. They refine the description of a position, and the Nakshatra of the Moon at birth fixes the start of the main timing system. ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.5","s":"module-01-lesson-05-introduction-to-the-27-nakshatras"}," introduces them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h10","text":"The Dashas"},
{"type":"para","text":["A ",{"t":"i","x":"Dasha"}," is a planetary period. In the ",{"t":"i","x":"Vimshottari"}," system, the one this course teaches, nine periods make a cycle of 120 years, and the cycle starts from the Nakshatra of the Moon at birth. A Dasha indicates which planet's themes may come into focus in a period. It is not a transit, and it is not a promise of events. ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 12","s":"module-12-introduction"}," teaches it."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h11","text":"Why Learn in a Systematic Sequence?"},
{"type":"para","text":"Many beginners meet astrology as a list of rules: this planet in that house means such and such. Rules of this kind are easy to memorise and easy to misuse, because each one leaves out the rest of the chart."},
{"type":"para","text":"Here is a concrete contrast. A popular rule of thumb attaches one fixed meaning to \"Mars in the 7th house\", the house traditionally linked with partnership. Take two hypothetical charts (not real people) that both have Mars in the 7th house. The rule treats them as the same. A framework does not."},
{"type":"table","caption":"One rule, two hypothetical charts","cols":3,"header":["Feature","Chart 1","Chart 2"],"rows":[["Lagna (rising sign)","Cancer (Karka)","Capricorn (Makara)"],["Sign of the 7th house","Capricorn","Cancer"],["Mars in that sign","Exaltation sign, the highest dignity","Debilitation sign, the lowest dignity"],["Houses ruled by Mars","5th and 10th","4th and 11th"],["Role of Mars for this Lagna","Yogakaraka","Functional malefic"]]},
{"type":"para","text":["Do not worry about the new words. A ",{"t":"i","x":"Yogakaraka"}," is a planet that rules both an angular house (the 4th, 7th or 10th) and a trine house (the 5th or 9th), a pairing traditionally seen as supportive. A functional malefic is a planet whose house lordships make it a more demanding influence for that Lagna. Module 5 teaches exaltation and debilitation, and Module 9 teaches Yogakaraka and functional roles. The point here is that the two charts already differ before we look at aspects, companions, divisional charts or periods, and even then no verdict follows."]},
{"type":"para","text":"A framework asks the right questions in the right order, so that each rule is read in context and each conclusion is held with care. The course teaches that order as a six-step method: Understand, Observe, Analyse, Establish Relationships, Verify, Interpret. The sequence is this course's own teaching device and not a classical procedure. The modules are cumulative, so it pays to study them in order."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h12","text":"How the Course Climbs"},
{"type":"para","text":"The fourteen modules climb from elementary concepts to integrated analysis. Module 1 teaches the chart itself: the zodiac, birth data, the Lagna and the two Indian drawing styles. Modules 2 to 4 teach the parts of a chart, which are the houses, planets and signs. Modules 5 and 6 teach planetary strength and the grouping of houses. Modules 7 and 8 teach selected beneficial and challenging Yogas. Module 9, the longest, shows how each of the twelve Ascendants changes the roles of the planets. Modules 10 to 13 add four tools: the links between factors (Module 10), divisional charts (Module 11), the Vimshottari Dasha (Module 12) and transits (Module 13). Module 14 gathers everything into a fifteen-step workflow and applies it in three case studies. A final assessment and a practical chart-reading examination close the course."},
{"type":"para","text":["The ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Roadmap","s":"course-home-roadmap"}," shows the stages, and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Detailed Course Syllabus","s":"course-home-syllabus"}," lists every lesson."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h13","text":"What You Will Be Able to Do"},
{"type":"para","text":"When you have finished the course, you will be able to do the following."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Set up a chart with clear settings, and read it in the North and South Indian styles."},{"text":"Name the signs, houses and Grahas, and give the lord of every house for any Lagna."},{"text":"Explain dignity and house groups, and test a chart for selected Yogas and Doshas with a verdict of present, verify or absent."},{"text":"Trace how planets and houses are linked, find a Navamsha sign, calculate Dasha periods and place a transit."},{"text":"Combine these steps into a qualified preliminary analysis, and say where it stops."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"a","x":"Course Objectives and Learning Outcomes","s":"course-home-objectives"}," states the fourteen outcomes and shows how each is taught and assessed."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h14","text":"Scope and Limits"},
{"type":"para","text":"A good course is honest about what it is."},
{"type":"callout","variant":"NOTE","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"This course teaches Vedic astrology (Jyotisha) as a traditional interpretive framework, not as a scientifically established method. No chart reading in this course is a prediction or a guarantee. Every interpretation is a hypothesis to examine. Nothing in this course is medical, legal or financial advice."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"An interpretive framework."}," The course does not claim that astrology is scientifically established, or that a chart causes events. It presents a body of traditional ideas, explains how they are applied and asks you to examine each one as a hypothesis."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Themes, not events."}," A reading names themes and tendencies. The course teaches no prediction of events and gives no dates for them."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Free will and effort."}," A chart is not treated as a script. Choice, effort, circumstances and the people around you all matter, and the course never presents a placement as fixed fate."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"No professional advice."}," Body associations are traditional symbolism, not health information. Questions about health, law or money belong with qualified professionals."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Limits of data and agreement."}," A reading is only as sound as the birth date, time and place behind it, and schools differ on some rules. The course says so wherever it matters."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Ethical use."}," Use another person's birth data only with their permission. Do not use a reading to frighten or pressure anyone, to claim certainty, or to replace professional advice. If a combination in your own chart worries you, remember that it is one factor in a framework and not a verdict, and consider talking with a qualified person."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":["Some subjects are named in the course but not taught, among them horoscope matching and the Jaimini, Tajika and Nadi systems. ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Level and Intended Audience","s":"course-home-course-level"}," lists them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h15","text":"Where to Begin"},
{"type":"para","text":"Take these pages in the order of the course menu before Module 1."},
{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"About the Instructors and Course Team","s":"course-home-instructor"},": who presents the course, its lineage and how it is taught."],"ordered":true,"n":1},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"How to Use This Course","s":"course-home-how-to-use"},": a study routine and a progress checklist."],"ordered":true,"n":2},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Prerequisites","s":"course-home-prerequisites"},": the skills and tools that help."],"ordered":true,"n":3},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Course Level and Intended Audience","s":"course-home-course-level"},": whether the course suits you."],"ordered":true,"n":4},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Course Roadmap","s":"course-home-roadmap"},": how each stage builds on the last."],"ordered":true,"n":5},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Course Conventions and Instructional Assumptions","s":"course-home-conventions-and-assumptions"},": the defaults and notation used everywhere."],"ordered":true,"n":6}]},
{"type":"para","text":["Then open the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 1 Introduction","s":"module-01-introduction"},". Work slowly, write things down, and treat each reading as a question to examine."]}
]},
"course-home-objectives":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"Why Outcomes Matter"},
{"type":"para","text":"A learning outcome says what you can do, not what you have read. \"Understand dignity\" cannot be checked. \"State a planet's dignity at a given sign and degree\" can. The fourteen outcomes below are written with action verbs so that you, the quizzes and the examinations can all check the same thing. The Student Workbook uses the same fourteen for its rating sheets."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"The Fourteen Learning Outcomes"},
{"type":"para","text":"By the end of the course you will be able to do the following."},
{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Explain"}," the basic structure of a Vedic horoscope: what a ",{"t":"i","x":"Kundali"}," shows, which settings it needs, and how the same chart looks in the North and South Indian styles."],"ordered":true,"n":1},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Identify"}," the twelve ",{"t":"i","x":"Rashis"},", twelve ",{"t":"i","x":"Bhavas"}," and nine ",{"t":"i","x":"Grahas"},", including Rahu and Ketu, and give the sign and lord of every house for any ",{"t":"i","x":"Lagna"},"."],"ordered":true,"n":2},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Explain"}," the significance of houses, planets and signs: give the core significations of each, tell a house from a sign, and tell ownership from placement."],"ordered":true,"n":3},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Explain"}," planetary dignity and basic strength: state a planet's dignity at a given sign and degree, and say why dignity alone does not settle a result."],"ordered":true,"n":4},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Classify"}," the houses by function: list every group a house belongs to, such as Kendra, Trikona, Upachaya or Dusthana, and say why a class is a framework and not a verdict."],"ordered":true,"n":5},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Recognise"}," selected auspicious and challenging Yogas: test a chart against the formation rule of each selected Yoga and give a verdict of present, verify or absent, with the reason."],"ordered":true,"n":6},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Explain"}," functional lordship for the twelve Ascendants: for any Lagna, name each house lord and class each planet as Yogakaraka, functional benefic, functional malefic or mixed-neutral."],"ordered":true,"n":7},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Analyse"}," planetary and house relationships: trace the links between planets, between houses, between planets and houses, and between planets and Nakshatras, and sort them as supporting, contradicting or neutral."],"ordered":true,"n":8},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Explain"}," the purpose of divisional charts: say what a ",{"t":"i","x":"Varga"}," is, calculate a ",{"t":"i","x":"Navamsha"}," sign, and say why a divisional chart is never read alone."],"ordered":true,"n":9},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Explain"}," the structure of the ",{"t":"i","x":"Vimshottari Dasha"},": name the nine periods with their years (120 in all), and calculate the balance of the first ",{"t":"i","x":"Mahadasha"}," and one ",{"t":"i","x":"Antardasha"},"."],"ordered":true,"n":10},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Interpret"}," the general role of transits: count a transiting planet's house from the Moon sign and the Lagna, use the ",{"t":"i","x":"Gochara"}," tables, and write a qualified statement that keeps natal, Dasha and transit factors apart."],"ordered":true,"n":11},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Combine"}," several factors in a structured preliminary analysis: follow the fifteen-step workflow, weigh supporting and contradicting indicators, and write a qualified five-part interpretation."],"ordered":true,"n":12},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practise"}," interpretation on three public-figure case studies: apply the workflow to the charts of Indira Gandhi, Mahatma Gandhi and Amitabh Bachchan, label uncertain birth data as provisional, and claim no cause between a chart and a life."],"ordered":true,"n":13},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Recognise"}," the limits of isolated rules and avoid deterministic conclusions: rewrite a deterministic sentence in hedged wording, and state what a given reading cannot tell."],"ordered":true,"n":14}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"Where Each Outcome Is Taught and Assessed"},
{"type":"para","text":"In the table, \"Section\" means a section of the Final Assessment, and \"Examination\" means the Practical Chart-Reading Examination."},
{"type":"table","caption":"The fourteen outcomes, the modules that teach them and the assessments that check them","cols":4,"header":["No.","Outcome in brief","Taught in","Checked by"],"rows":[["1","Structure of a horoscope","Module 1","Module 1 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section A; Examination Exercise A"],["2","Rashis, Bhavas, Grahas","Modules 1 to 4","Quizzes of Modules 1 to 4; Final Assessment Section A; Examination Exercise A"],["3","Significance of houses, planets, signs","Modules 2 to 4","Quizzes of Modules 2 to 4; Final Assessment Section A"],["4","Dignity and basic strength","Module 5","Module 5 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section B; Examination Exercise B"],["5","House classification","Module 6","Module 6 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section B"],["6","Auspicious and challenging Yogas","Modules 7 and 8","Quizzes of Modules 7 and 8; Final Assessment Section C; Examination Exercise B"],["7","Lordship for twelve Ascendants","Module 9","Module 9 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section D; Examination Exercise A"],["8","Planetary and house relationships","Module 10","Module 10 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section D; Examination Exercise B"],["9","Divisional charts","Module 11","Module 11 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section E"],["10","Vimshottari Dasha","Module 12","Module 12 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section E"],["11","Transits","Module 13","Module 13 quiz and completion test; Final Assessment Section E"],["12","Structured preliminary analysis","Module 14, with Modules 9 and 10","Module 14 practical and quiz; Final Assessment Section F; Examination Exercise C"],["13","Three case studies","Module 14","Student activity and answer guide of each case study; Workbook Worksheets 20.1 to 20.3"],["14","Limits of isolated rules","Modules 7, 8 and 14","Careful-wording questions in the completion tests; Final Assessment Section F; Examination Exercise C"]]},
{"type":"para","text":"Outcome 14 is not confined to three modules. Every lesson has a section on common mistakes, and most completion tests ask you to rewrite an overconfident sentence in careful words."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"How the Outcomes Are Assessed"},
{"type":"para","text":"Each outcome is checked in more than one way, because a single test can be passed by memory alone."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Module quiz and completion test."}," Every module ends with a quiz (Part A) that checks whether you can use the lessons, and a completion test (Part B) that asks for reasoning in new situations. Answers and the lesson to revisit are in the module Answer Key."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical exercise."}," Each module has a guided worked practical and exercises, which you compare with the Answer Key."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Final Assessment."}," Fifty questions and 100 marks, in six sections that follow the modules from the structure of a chart to integrated interpretation. See the ",{"t":"a","x":"Final Assessment","s":"assessment-final-assessment"},"."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical Chart-Reading Examination."}," Three exercises of rising difficulty (identification, analysis and integration), marked on accuracy, method, reasoning, terminology and appropriate qualifications. See the ",{"t":"a","x":"Practical Chart-Reading Examination","s":"assessment-practical-chart-reading-examination"},"."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Case studies."}," The student activity of each Module 14 case study asks for independent analysis, with an answer guide."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":["In interpretive questions the wording counts as well as the chart facts. A sentence that states an event as certain, or gives medical, legal or financial advice, loses its mark. Pass marks are recommended settings that the LMS administrator may adjust; see ",{"t":"a","x":"Assessment Framework and Completion Criteria","s":"assessment-framework-and-completion-criteria"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"Self-Rating Checklist"},
{"type":"para","text":["Rate yourself on each outcome three times: at the start, at the midpoint of the course and at the end. Use the scale below, and rate each line by its weakest part. Worksheet 1.3 in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 1","s":"workbook-01-goals-and-terminology"}," and Worksheet 21.1 in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 6","s":"workbook-06-case-studies-checklists-and-journal"}," record your ratings on the same scale and the same fourteen lines."]},
{"type":"table","caption":"The rating scale","cols":2,"header":["Rating","Meaning"],"rows":[["1","Not yet: I cannot do it, or I only recognise the words"],["2","With help: I can do it with the lesson open"],["3","On my own: I can do it with my notes"],["4","Secure: I can do it from memory and explain it to another learner"]]},
{"type":"table","caption":"Rate yourself on each outcome (write 1, 2, 3 or 4 in the last three columns)","cols":5,"header":["No. and outcome","I can show it by","Start","Midway","End"],"rows":[["1. Structure","Drawing one hypothetical chart in both styles","","",""],["2. Rashis, Bhavas, Grahas","Filling a blank template, with sign lords","","",""],["3. Significance","Giving two themes and one limit for a house, a planet and a sign","","",""],["4. Dignity","Stating a dignity at a given degree, and why it is not strength","","",""],["5. House classes","Listing every group of the 6th house","","",""],["6. Yogas","Testing one auspicious and one challenging Yoga, with verdicts","","",""],["7. Lordship","Naming each house lord for one Lagna, with any Yogakaraka","","",""],["8. Relationships","Listing the conjunction, aspect, exchange and lord links of a chart","","",""],["9. Divisional charts","Finding a Navamsha sign by hand, and saying why D9 is never read alone","","",""],["10. Dasha","Calculating a first Mahadasha balance","","",""],["11. Transits","Giving Saturn's house from the Moon sign on a stated date","","",""],["12. Analysis","Completing the fifteen-step worksheet and a five-part reading","","",""],["13. Case studies","Finishing a case-study activity and checking the guide","","",""],["14. Limits","Rewriting three deterministic sentences in hedged words","","",""]]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"checklist":true,"id":"ck1","items":[{"text":"I rated every line by its weakest part.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.1"},{"text":"I tried the task in the second column before rating any line 3 or 4, and I lowered the rating when I could not finish the task.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.2"},{"text":"For every line I rated 1 or 2, I named a lesson to revisit.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.3"},{"text":"My end ratings agree with my quiz and examination results. If they disagree, I trust the results and recheck the ratings.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.4"}]},
{"type":"para","text":"An honest rating of 1 at the start is normal for a complete beginner. It is a starting point and not a weakness."}
]},
"course-home-syllabus":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"About This Syllabus"},
{"type":"para","text":["This syllabus is the detailed plan of ",{"t":"i","x":"Basics of Vedic Astrology: A Complete Beginner's Course"},", presented by ASTROSUTRAS by Anoopam Shukla. It lists the 14 modules in teaching order. Its titles and numbers match the lesson pages, so you can also use it as an index."]},
{"type":"para","text":["The course teaches Vedic astrology (",{"t":"i","x":"Jyotisha"},") as a traditional interpretive framework and not as a scientifically established method. No reading is a prediction or a guarantee, and every interpretation is a hypothesis to examine. Nothing in the course is medical, legal or financial advice."]},
{"type":"para","text":["Every module entry opens with a line that gives the suggested time and the course outcomes the module serves. The outcome numbers (1 to 14) come from ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Objectives and Learning Outcomes","s":"course-home-objectives"},". The entry then has five labels."]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description"}," says what the module covers."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered"}," lists the lessons with their titles. Each title links to its page."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes"}," states what you will be able to do. Outcome 14, recognising the limits of isolated rules, runs through every module as the habit of treating a reading as a hypothesis, so it is tagged only where a module teaches it directly. The tags follow the table on that page."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity"}," describes the Practical Exercise and the matching Student Workbook pages."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method"}," names the quiz, the completion test, the answer key and the checklist. Each module has a Part A quiz and a Part B completion test. The suggested pass mark is 60 percent of each part, a recommended setting that the LMS administrator may adjust."]}]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"NOTE","lines":[{"k":"p","text":["The modules are cumulative, so study them in order. See ",{"t":"a","x":"How to Use This Course","s":"course-home-how-to-use"}," and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Roadmap","s":"course-home-roadmap"},"."]}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"The 14 Modules at a Glance"},
{"type":"table","caption":"The 14 modules, their lessons and suggested hours","cols":4,"header":["Module","Title","Lessons","Suggested hours"],"rows":[["1",[{"t":"a","x":"What Is a Kundali or Horoscope?","s":"module-01-introduction"}],"8","6"],["2",[{"t":"a","x":"Signification of Houses","s":"module-02-introduction"}],"6","6"],["3",[{"t":"a","x":"Signification of Planets","s":"module-03-introduction"}],"7","7"],["4",[{"t":"a","x":"Signification of Signs","s":"module-04-introduction"}],"5","6"],["5",[{"t":"a","x":"Planetary Dignity","s":"module-05-introduction"}],"5","6"],["6",[{"t":"a","x":"Classification of Houses","s":"module-06-introduction"}],"4","5"],["7",[{"t":"a","x":"Good Yogas in Vedic Astrology","s":"module-07-introduction"}],"7","7"],["8",[{"t":"a","x":"Challenging Yogas and Doshas","s":"module-08-introduction"}],"7","6"],["9",[{"t":"a","x":"All Twelve Ascendants","s":"module-09-introduction"}],"5 lessons and 12 Ascendant chapters","16"],["10",[{"t":"a","x":"Sambandha: Planetary and House Relationships","s":"module-10-introduction"}],"6","7"],["11",[{"t":"a","x":"Divisional Charts and Their Significations","s":"module-11-introduction"}],"7","7"],["12",[{"t":"a","x":"Vimshottari Dasha and Its Use","s":"module-12-introduction"}],"5","7"],["13",[{"t":"a","x":"Transits: Gochara","s":"module-13-introduction"}],"5","6"],["14",[{"t":"a","x":"Putting It All Together: Integrated Chart Analysis","s":"module-14-introduction"}],"2 lessons and 3 case studies","11"],["Total","14 modules","79 lessons, 12 chapters, 3 case studies","103"]]},
{"type":"para","text":"Every module also has a Practical Exercise, a Quiz and Completion Test, an Answer Key and a Summary. Their time is inside the hours shown. The hours are planning estimates, and each module introduction gives its own split, which can differ from this table by about an hour."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"Suggested 16-Week Pacing Plan"},
{"type":"para","text":"The plan spreads the course over 16 weeks. It is a suggestion and not a requirement. The course is self-paced, so you may go faster, go slower or pause between modules."},
{"type":"table","caption":"A suggested week-by-week plan","cols":3,"header":["Week","Study","Suggested hours"],"rows":[["1","Course Home pages, then Module 1: What Is a Kundali or Horoscope?","6"],["2","Module 2: Signification of Houses","6"],["3","Module 3: Signification of Planets","7"],["4","Module 4: Signification of Signs","6"],["5","Module 5: Planetary Dignity","6"],["6","Module 6: Classification of Houses","5"],["7","Module 7: Good Yogas in Vedic Astrology","7"],["8","Module 8: Challenging Yogas and Doshas","6"],["9","Module 9, first half: Lessons 9.1 to 9.4 and Ascendants 1 to 6 (Aries to Virgo)","8"],["10","Module 9, second half: Ascendants 7 to 12 (Libra to Pisces), Lesson 9.5, practical and quiz","8"],["11","Module 10: Sambandha: Planetary and House Relationships","7"],["12","Module 11: Divisional Charts and Their Significations","7"],["13","Module 12: Vimshottari Dasha and Its Use","7"],["14","Module 13: Transits: Gochara","6"],["15","Module 14: Putting It All Together: Integrated Chart Analysis","11"],["16","Final Assessment, Practical Chart-Reading Examination and Course Completion","Within the routine"]]},
{"type":"para","text":"Read the plan with these points in mind."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"The total is about 100 hours."}," The module hours add up to 103, and a routine of 6 to 7 hours a week over 16 weeks gives 96 to 112 hours."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Nothing is added on top."}," The final assessment, the practical examination and the work in the Student Workbook are fitted into the same 16 weeks."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Some weeks run heavier."}," Weeks 9, 10 and 15 exceed the 6 to 7 hour routine, and week 6 is lighter. If week 15 is too much, add a week before the assessments."]}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"Module 1: What Is a Kundali or Horoscope?"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding the Structure of North Indian and South Indian Horoscope Charts"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 6 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 1 and 2."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 1 teaches the chart itself, before any interpretation. You learn what a ",{"t":"i","x":"Kundali"}," is, which zodiac it uses, which birth facts it needs and how its twelve signs, twelve houses and nine planets fit together. You then read one chart in the two common Indian styles and convert it from one style to the other. Positions are facts of calculation, and interpretation begins in Module 2."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.1: What Is a Kundali?","s":"module-01-lesson-01-what-is-a-kundali"}," - Kundali, ",{"t":"i","x":"Janma Kundali"}," (birth chart) and horoscope; the six-step method."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.2: The Sidereal Zodiac and the Ayanamsha","s":"module-01-lesson-02-sidereal-zodiac-and-ayanamsha"}," - Tropical and sidereal zodiacs; the ",{"t":"i","x":"Ayanamsha"},", the offset between them."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.3: Birth Data and How a Chart Is Calculated","s":"module-01-lesson-03-birth-data-and-chart-calculation"}," - Date, time and place; clock time to Universal Time; grading a birth time."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.4: Lagna, Rashis, Bhavas and Grahas","s":"module-01-lesson-04-lagna-rashis-bhavas-and-grahas"}," - The ",{"t":"i","x":"Lagna"}," (rising sign), ",{"t":"i","x":"Rashis"}," (signs), ",{"t":"i","x":"Bhavas"}," (houses) and ",{"t":"i","x":"Grahas"}," (planets)."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.5: Introduction to the 27 Nakshatras","s":"module-01-lesson-05-introduction-to-the-27-nakshatras"}," - The 27 ",{"t":"i","x":"Nakshatras"}," (divisions of the zodiac) and their quarters, the ",{"t":"i","x":"Padas"},"."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.6: Reading the North Indian Chart","s":"module-01-lesson-06-reading-the-north-indian-chart"}," - The diamond style, with fixed houses."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.7: Reading the South Indian Chart","s":"module-01-lesson-07-reading-the-south-indian-chart"}," - The square-grid style, with fixed signs."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.8: Comparing the Two Formats and Converting Between Them","s":"module-01-lesson-08-comparing-north-and-south-indian-charts"}," - A five-step conversion."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Explain what a Kundali is, and separate positions from interpretations."},{"text":"Convert a tropical longitude to a sidereal one with the Lahiri Ayanamsha, and name the sign."},{"text":"Convert a birth time to Universal Time (UT), and grade it as documented, recollected or rectified."},{"text":"Find the sign, house and lord of any house for any Lagna, and the Moon's Nakshatra and Pada."},{"text":"Read a chart in both styles, and convert one into the other without changing any planet's sign or house."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 1 Practical Exercise","s":"module-01-practical-exercise"}," draws a hypothetical chart, Sample Horoscope B, in the South Indian style, converts it to the North Indian style, tests a later birth time and names the Lagna, Sun sign, Moon sign and Nakshatra. Blank templates are in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-01-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-01-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 1 Summary","s":"module-01-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"Module 2: Signification of Houses"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding the Twelve Bhavas and Their Life Domains"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 6 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 2 and 3."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 2 teaches what the twelve houses stand for. A house is a division of the chart that names a group of related life matters, such as the body, money, home, work or partnership. You learn the Sanskrit name, core significations and supporting themes of each house, and you read the so-called difficult houses with their constructive sides. A house names a theme and not an event, and no house is read alone."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 2.1: How to Think About Bhavas","s":"module-02-lesson-01-how-to-think-about-bhavas"}," - House versus sign and planet; the house lord (",{"t":"i","x":"Bhavesh"},") versus the natural significator (",{"t":"i","x":"Karaka"},"); five checks."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 2.2: Houses 1 to 4","s":"module-02-lesson-02-houses-1-to-4"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Tanu"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Dhana"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Sahaja"}," and ",{"t":"i","x":"Sukha Bhava"},"."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 2.3: Houses 5 to 8","s":"module-02-lesson-03-houses-5-to-8"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Putra"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Ari"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Yuvati"}," and ",{"t":"i","x":"Randhra Bhava"},"."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 2.4: Houses 9 to 12","s":"module-02-lesson-04-houses-9-to-12"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Dharma"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Karma"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Labha"}," and ",{"t":"i","x":"Vyaya Bhava"},"."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 2.5: The Twelve-House Reference Table","s":"module-02-lesson-05-the-twelve-house-reference-table"}," - Looking up any house; turning questions into houses."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 2.6: How Houses Work Together","s":"module-02-lesson-06-how-houses-work-together"}," - Opposite houses, trines and the five classic combinations."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Name the twelve houses in Sanskrit, and give the core and secondary significations of each."},{"text":"Separate a house from its sign and planets, and the house lord from the natural significator."},{"text":"Apply five checks (sign, lord, occupants, aspects and natural significator) to one house and write a hedged reading."},{"text":"Read the 6th, 8th and 12th houses with their constructive sides as well as their challenging ones."},{"text":"Turn an everyday question into the right house, with a reason."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 2 Practical Exercise","s":"module-02-practical-exercise"}," maps twenty-six everyday questions to houses, in three rounds of growing subtlety. You then read two hypothetical charts, Sample Horoscope C and Sample Horoscope D, with the five checks. The twelve-house worksheet of ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"}," supports the work."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-02-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-02-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 2 Summary","s":"module-02-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h6","text":"Module 3: Signification of Planets"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding the Nature, Functions, and Karakatwas of the Grahas"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 7 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 2 and 3."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 3 teaches the nine Grahas: the Sun, Moon, Mars, Mercury, Jupiter, Venus and Saturn, and the lunar nodes Rahu and Ketu. For each you learn its nature, its ",{"t":"i","x":"Karakatwas"}," (the themes it traditionally signifies), its dignity signs and the houses it aspects. The central skill is to keep a planet's natural nature apart from its functional role, which depends on the houses it owns for a given Lagna."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.1: How to Read a Graha","s":"module-03-lesson-01-how-to-read-a-graha"}," - Nature versus functional role; the planet checklist."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.2: The Sun and the Moon","s":"module-03-lesson-02-sun-and-moon"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Surya"}," and ",{"t":"i","x":"Chandra"},"; the Moon's phase."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.3: Mars and Mercury","s":"module-03-lesson-03-mars-and-mercury"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Mangala"}," and ",{"t":"i","x":"Budha"},"; Mercury's company."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.4: Jupiter and Venus","s":"module-03-lesson-04-jupiter-and-venus"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Guru"}," and ",{"t":"i","x":"Shukra"},"; why a natural benefic is not always beneficial."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.5: Saturn","s":"module-03-lesson-05-saturn"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Shani"},"; why \"always bad\" is a misconception."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.6: Rahu and Ketu","s":"module-03-lesson-06-rahu-and-ketu"}," - The nodes as calculated points; the mean node, the course default."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 3.7: The Nine Grahas Compared","s":"module-03-lesson-07-the-nine-grahas-compared"}," - Four reference tables and a decision flow."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Separate a Graha's natural nature and Karakatwas from its functional role for a Lagna."},{"text":["Classify the nine Grahas as ",{"t":"i","x":"Saumya"}," (benefic) or ",{"t":"i","x":"Krura"}," (malefic), working out the Moon's class from its phase and Mercury's from its company."]},{"text":"Describe each Graha with one template, labelling each Karakatwa as classical or modern."},{"text":"State the houses each Graha aspects, including the 5th, 7th and 9th for the nodes under the course convention, and name the views of other schools."},{"text":"Read one Graha of a hypothetical chart with the planet checklist."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 3 Practical Exercise","s":"module-03-practical-exercise"}," matches the nine Grahas to their themes, finds the Moon's phase and Mercury's company, classifies dignity at eight positions and reads four Grahas of a hypothetical chart. It ends by comparing the same Grahas under two Lagnas. Use the nine-Graha worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-03-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-03-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 3 Summary","s":"module-03-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h7","text":"Module 4: Signification of Signs"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"The Twelve Rashis and Their Qualities"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 6 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 2 and 3."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 4 teaches the twelve Rashis, each a 30-degree stretch of the zodiac. In the course's memory aid, the Graha is who acts, the Bhava is where in life, and the Rashi is how the action tends to be coloured. Each sign has a card with seven labels. The module keeps three kinds of material apart: classical facts, traditional symbolism (body associations, which are not medical information) and modern teaching conventions (behavioural keywords)."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 4.1: The Structure of the Zodiac","s":"module-04-lesson-01-the-structure-of-the-zodiac"}," - Element, modality, polarity, direction and lord."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 4.2: Mesha to Karka (Aries to Cancer)","s":"module-04-lesson-02-mesha-to-karka"}," - The cards of the first four signs."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 4.3: Simha to Vrischika (Leo to Scorpio)","s":"module-04-lesson-03-simha-to-vrischika"}," - The cards of the next four signs."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 4.4: Dhanu to Meena (Sagittarius to Pisces)","s":"module-04-lesson-04-dhanu-to-meena"}," - The cards of the last four signs."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 4.5: Signs in Practice","s":"module-04-lesson-05-signs-in-practice"}," - Sign versus house; sign lord versus house lord; a six-step reading."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"List the twelve Rashis in order with their lords, and find the sign of any longitude."},{"text":"Classify any sign by element, modality, polarity and direction."},{"text":"Describe each sign from its card, labelling each statement as classical, symbolic or modern convention."},{"text":"Separate a sign from a house and a sign lord from a house lord, and find the sign and lord of any house for any Lagna."},{"text":"Read a Graha in a sign in six steps and write one hedged sentence."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 4 Practical Exercise","s":"module-04-practical-exercise"}," turns longitudes into signs, reads twenty Grahas in signs, finds the sign on a house for different Lagnas and works through a whole hypothetical horoscope. Build your own reference on the twelve-Rashi worksheet of ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-04-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-04-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 4 Summary","s":"module-04-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h8","text":"Module 5: Planetary Dignity"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Exaltation, Debilitation, Own Signs, Moolatrikona, and Other Dignity Concepts"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 6 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 4."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 5 asks how well a given sign suits a given planet. Tradition grades that suitability from exaltation (",{"t":"i","x":"Uchcha"},") to debilitation (",{"t":"i","x":"Neecha"},"), and the course calls it dignity. You learn the states, planetary friendship, and what dignity leaves out: strength, combustion, retrogression and ",{"t":"i","x":"Neechabhanga"},", the easing of a debilitation. Dignity describes a condition and not a verdict, so an exalted planet is not automatically helpful."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 5.1: Exaltation and Debilitation","s":"module-05-lesson-01-exaltation-and-debilitation"}," - Signs and deepest points of the seven classical planets."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 5.2: Own Sign (Swakshetra) and Moolatrikona","s":"module-05-lesson-02-own-sign-and-moolatrikona"}," - Own signs; ",{"t":"i","x":"Moolatrikona"},", a high-dignity stretch of one sign."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 5.3: Friendly, Neutral and Enemy Relationships","s":"module-05-lesson-03-friends-neutrals-and-enemies"}," - Natural, temporary and compound friendship."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 5.4: Dignity Versus Strength: Dig Bala, Shadbala, Combustion and Retrogression","s":"module-05-lesson-04-dignity-versus-strength"}," - Why dignity is not strength; combustion and retrogression as separate modifiers."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 5.5: Neechabhanga and Reading Dignity in Context","s":"module-05-lesson-05-neechabhanga-and-reading-dignity-in-context"}," - Testing a cancellation; a six-point routine."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"State the exaltation sign, debilitation sign and deepest point of each classical planet."},{"text":"Classify a planet at a given sign and degree as exalted, Moolatrikona or own sign, including at range boundaries."},{"text":"Work out natural, temporary and compound friendship."},{"text":["Explain why dignity is not strength, and apply ",{"t":"i","x":"Dig Bala"}," (directional strength), combustion orbs and retrogression."]},{"text":"Test a debilitated planet for Neechabhanga, and weigh dignity with lordship, house and aspects."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 5 Practical Exercise","s":"module-05-practical-exercise"}," classifies ten positions, compares one exalted planet in two charts that differ in Lagna, lordship and company, runs four combustion checks and tests a debilitated planet for Neechabhanga. Use the dignity worksheets in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-05-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (30 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-05-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 5 Summary","s":"module-05-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h9","text":"Module 6: Classification of Houses"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Kendra, Trikona, Upachaya, Dusthana, and Other House Groups"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 5 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 5."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 6 sorts the twelve houses into groups that share a nature: the angular houses (",{"t":"i","x":"Kendra"},"), the trine houses (",{"t":"i","x":"Trikona"},"), the houses of increase (",{"t":"i","x":"Upachaya"},"), the challenging houses (",{"t":"i","x":"Dusthana"},"), the ",{"t":"i","x":"Maraka"}," houses (the 2nd and 7th, a technical label) and the four aims of life. It then joins the groups to house lordship, so that you can say what a planet tends to do for a given Lagna. A group describes a house, whoever stands in it. It is a framework and not a verdict."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 6.1: Kendra and Trikona Houses","s":"module-06-lesson-01-kendra-and-trikona"}," - Why the 1st house belongs to both."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 6.2: Upachaya, Dusthana and Trishadaya Houses","s":"module-06-lesson-02-upachaya-dusthana-and-trishadaya"}," - Houses of growth and challenge; a first look at Vipareeta Raja Yoga."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 6.3: Maraka Houses and the Dharma, Artha, Kama, Moksha Groups","s":"module-06-lesson-03-maraka-and-the-purushartha-groups"}," - The Maraka label and its limits; the four aim groups."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 6.4: Classification and Lordship: Frameworks, Not Verdicts","s":"module-06-lesson-04-classification-and-lordship"}," - The master table; classifying a planet's role in five steps."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Name the houses of each group and find them in any chart by counting from the Lagna."},{"text":"Explain why the 1st house is both a Kendra and a Trikona."},{"text":"Describe the Dusthana houses with their constructive sides, and the Maraka label with its limits."},{"text":"Classify a planet's role for a Lagna in five steps."},{"text":"Give six reasons why classification is a framework and not a verdict."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 6 Practical Exercise","s":"module-06-practical-exercise"}," asks you to sort the twelve houses with a reason for every group, solve overlap puzzles, read the groups in a hypothetical chart and turn house ownership into a planet's role for two Lagnas. The house-classification worksheet is in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-06-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-06-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 6 Summary","s":"module-06-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h10","text":"Module 7: Good Yogas in Vedic Astrology"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding Auspicious Planetary Combinations"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 7 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 6 and 14."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 7 introduces the ",{"t":"i","x":"Yoga"},", a named combination of planets, signs or houses that tradition links with stated themes. Tradition calls most of the Yogas here auspicious, meaning associated with support or good fortune. Each Yoga is studied with the same eight-point profile, and each test ends in a verdict: present, verify or absent. A Yoga is a potential and not a promise, and the rule you test is the course default, with school variants named."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.1: What Is a Yoga?","s":"module-07-lesson-01-what-is-a-yoga"}," - Formation, strength and manifestation; the verdict words."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.2: Gaja Kesari Yoga and Budha-Aditya Yoga","s":"module-07-lesson-02-gaja-kesari-and-budha-aditya-yoga"}," - Counting a Kendra from the Moon; judging combustion."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.3: The Pancha Mahapurusha Yogas","s":"module-07-lesson-03-pancha-mahapurusha-yogas"}," - Five Yogas, each from a planet in its own or exaltation sign in a Kendra from the Lagna."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.4: Dharma-Karmadhipati Yoga and Raja Yoga","s":"module-07-lesson-04-dharma-karmadhipati-and-raja-yoga"}," - Pairing Kendra lords with Trikona lords; the ",{"t":"i","x":"Yogakaraka"},", a single planet that lords both."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.5: Dhana Yoga and Lakshmi Yoga","s":"module-07-lesson-05-dhana-yoga-and-lakshmi-yoga"}," - Linking the resource-house lords; testing the 9th lord."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.6: Parivartana Yoga and Neechabhanga Raja Yoga","s":"module-07-lesson-06-parivartana-and-neechabhanga-raja-yoga"}," - Sign exchange (",{"t":"i","x":"Parivartana"},"); cancelled debilitation."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.7: Verifying a Yoga Step by Step","s":"module-07-lesson-07-verifying-a-yoga-step-by-step"}," - A seven-step routine and a responsible conclusion."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Explain what a Yoga is, and keep formation apart from strength and manifestation."},{"text":"Test a chart for Gaja Kesari, Budha-Aditya and the five Pancha Mahapurusha Yogas."},{"text":"Find the Kendra lords, Trikona lords and Yogakaraka of a Lagna, and test their pairs for Raja Yoga."},{"text":"Apply the course rules for Dhana Yoga and Lakshmi Yoga, classify a Parivartana and test a debilitated planet for Neechabhanga Raja Yoga."},{"text":"Run the seven-step verification routine and word a conclusion responsibly."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 7 Practical Exercise","s":"module-07-practical-exercise"}," gives fourteen hypothetical charts. For each you decide present, verify or absent and write your reason, and a final exercise asks for one careful written conclusion. Use the Yoga worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-07-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (30 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-07-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 7 Summary","s":"module-07-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h11","text":"Module 8: Challenging Yogas and Doshas"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding Difficult Combinations Without Fear-Based Interpretation"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 6 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 6 and 14."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 8 explains that traditional astrology may classify some combinations as adverse under specified conditions. A ",{"t":"i","x":"Dosha"},", literally a fault or defect, is such a placement, and the same sources usually list conditions that remove or soften it. Five emphases run through the module: a challenging combination does not establish inevitable suffering, one placement cannot decide a whole area of life, schools differ, fear-based claims have no place, and a remedy is a tradition-specific practice and not a guaranteed solution."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.1: Reading Difficult Combinations Without Fear","s":"module-08-lesson-01-reading-difficult-combinations-without-fear"}," - The five emphases and the eight-point profile."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.2: Kemadruma Yoga and Grahan Yoga","s":"module-08-lesson-02-kemadruma-and-grahan-yoga"}," - The Moon's neighbours; the Sun or Moon with a node."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.3: Guru-Chandala, Shakata and Daridra Yoga","s":"module-08-lesson-03-guru-chandala-shakata-and-daridra-yoga"}," - Jupiter with Rahu; the Moon from Jupiter; the 11th lord."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.4: Challenging Forms of Parivartana","s":"module-08-lesson-04-challenging-forms-of-parivartana"}," - Khala and Dainya exchanges; Vipareeta Raja Yoga, a Dusthana lord standing in a Dusthana."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.5: Mangal Dosha (Kuja Dosha)","s":"module-08-lesson-05-mangal-dosha"}," - The six-house rule for Mars, with its exceptions."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.6: Kala Sarpa Yoga","s":"module-08-lesson-06-kala-sarpa-yoga"}," - A modern label whose definition varies."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 8.7: Assessing Context and Understanding Remedies","s":"module-08-lesson-07-assessing-context-and-remedies"}," - A seven-step routine; remedies without promises."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Explain what a Dosha is, and profile any combination with the eight-point profile and the verdicts present, verify and absent."},{"text":"Test a chart for Kemadruma, Grahan, Guru-Chandala, Shakata and Daridra Yoga, and say how firmly each name can be stated."},{"text":"Classify a sign exchange as Maha, Khala or Dainya Parivartana, and test the Dusthana lords for Vipareeta Raja Yoga."},{"text":"Apply the six-house rule of Mangal Dosha, and decide by degree whether the seven planets lie on one side of the Rahu-Ketu axis."},{"text":"Weigh a combination in the whole chart, and rewrite a fear-based sentence in responsible wording."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 8 Practical Exercise","s":"module-08-practical-exercise"}," presents charts that look alarming at first sight. You test each combination, find what cancels or softens it and say only what the evidence supports. You also rewrite fearful statements and change one fact to see how a verdict moves. Use the Yoga worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-08-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-08-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 8 Summary","s":"module-08-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h12","text":"Module 9: All Twelve Ascendants"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Functional Planetary Lordship and the Blending of Rashis with Bhavas"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 16 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 7 and 12."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 9 is the longest module. The Lagna decides which sign fills each house, and so which planet rules each house, so the same planet plays different roles for different Lagnas. A planet's ",{"t":"i","x":"functional nature"}," is the tendency it takes from the houses it owns for one Lagna. Four lessons teach the method, twelve chapters apply it to the twelve Lagnas, and a final lesson compares them."]},
{"type":"para","text":"Every Ascendant chapter has six parts: A. House Ownership Table; B. Planetary Lordship Table; C. Blending of Rashi over Bhava, built with the formula House Theme + Sign Qualities + House Lord + Planetary Condition; D. Ascendant-Specific Analysis; E. Worked Examples, with two hypothetical charts; F. Revision Exercise."},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 9.1: The Lagna and the Whole-Sign House Sequence","s":"module-09-lesson-01-lagna-and-the-whole-sign-sequence"}," - Which sign falls in which house, and why whole-sign houses."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 9.2: House Ownership and Functional Nature","s":"module-09-lesson-02-house-ownership-and-functional-nature"}," - The lordship matrix and rules for classifying a planet."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 9.3: Yogakaraka, Maraka and Other Lordship Roles","s":"module-09-lesson-03-yogakaraka-maraka-and-relationship-rules"}," - The six Lagnas that have a Yogakaraka."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 9.4: The Rashi-Over-Bhava Method","s":"module-09-lesson-04-the-rashi-over-bhava-method"}," - The four-term formula."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 1: Aries (Mesha Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-01-aries-mesha-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 2: Taurus (Vrishabha Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-02-taurus-vrishabha-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 3: Gemini (Mithuna Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-03-gemini-mithuna-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 4: Cancer (Karka Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-04-cancer-karka-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 5: Leo (Simha Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-05-leo-simha-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 6: Virgo (Kanya Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-06-virgo-kanya-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 7: Libra (Tula Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-07-libra-tula-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 8: Scorpio (Vrischika Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-08-scorpio-vrischika-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 9: Sagittarius (Dhanu Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-09-sagittarius-dhanu-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 10: Capricorn (Makara Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-10-capricorn-makara-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 11: Aquarius (Kumbha Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-11-aquarius-kumbha-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Ascendant 12: Pisces (Meena Lagna)","s":"module-09-ascendant-12-pisces-meena-lagna"}]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 9.5: Comparing the Twelve Ascendants","s":"module-09-comparing-the-twelve-ascendants"}," - Patterns across all twelve Lagnas."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Find the sign and the lord of any house for any Lagna, counting forwards from a house and in reverse from a sign."},{"text":"Classify the seven planets of a Lagna as Yogakaraka, functional benefic, functional malefic or mixed-neutral, flag the Maraka lords, and give the reason from the houses owned."},{"text":"Name the six Lagnas that have a Yogakaraka and its planet, and explain why the other six have none."},{"text":"Apply the four-term formula to a house and write a hedged preliminary framework."},{"text":"Read a hypothetical chart at the lordship level, listing the Kendra-Trikona lord pairs and the Lagna lord's relationships."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 9 Practical Exercise","s":"module-09-practical-exercise"}," rebuilds house lords for every Lagna, classifies planets, tests claims about the Yogakaraka, reads five hypothetical charts at the lordship level and writes one framework. Each chapter also ends with its own revision exercise. Use the Ascendant house-lord tables in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-09-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). The ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-09-answer-key"}," also answers the twelve chapter exercises. Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 9 Summary","s":"module-09-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h13","text":"Module 10: Sambandha: Planetary and House Relationships"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"How Astrological Factors Work Together"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 7 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 8 and 12."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 10 teaches how the parts of a chart are tied together. ",{"t":"i","x":"Sambandha"}," is the Sanskrit word for relationship, and the course uses it for the links between planets, houses and Nakshatras. There are four families of link: planet to planet (",{"t":"i","x":"Graha ka Graha se"},"), house to house (",{"t":"i","x":"Bhava ka Bhava se"},"), planet to house (",{"t":"i","x":"Graha ka Bhava se"},") and planet to Nakshatra (",{"t":"i","x":"Graha ka Nakshatra se"},"). A nine-point framework then gathers the facts about one house before any opinion forms."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.1: What Is Sambandha?","s":"module-10-lesson-01-what-is-sambandha"}," - The four families in outline."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.2: Graha ka Graha Se (Planet to Planet)","s":"module-10-lesson-02-graha-ka-graha-se"}," - Conjunction (",{"t":"i","x":"Yuti"},"), aspect (",{"t":"i","x":"Drishti"},"), sign exchange, friendship and dispositor chains."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.3: Bhava ka Bhava Se (House to House)","s":"module-10-lesson-03-bhava-ka-bhava-se"}," - Ties between houses and between house lords; five classic pairs."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.4: Graha ka Bhava Se (Planet to House)","s":"module-10-lesson-04-graha-ka-bhava-se"}," - Occupation, lordship, aspect and natural significator."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.5: Graha ka Nakshatra Se (Planet and Nakshatra)","s":"module-10-lesson-05-graha-ka-nakshatra-se"}," - Nakshatra lords and chains of lords."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.6: Integrating the Relationships: A Nine-Point Framework","s":"module-10-lesson-06-integrating-relationships"}," - Sorting findings into supporting, contradicting and neutral."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Explain what Sambandha means, name its four families and say why a placement cannot be read without its links."},{"text":"Find the planet-to-planet links of a chart, counting aspects by sign, with Rahu and Ketu on the 5th, 7th and 9th signs by the course default."},{"text":"Count one house from another and trace the ties between house lords in the five classic pairs."},{"text":"List the links between a planet and a house, and weigh each by role and dignity."},{"text":"Find the Nakshatra, Pada and Nakshatra lord of every planet, and trace one house with the nine-point framework."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 10 Practical Exercise","s":"module-10-practical-exercise"}," traces the four families of Sambandha one at a time in three hypothetical charts, then combines them in the nine-point worksheet. Use ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 4","s":"workbook-04-sambandha-and-nakshatra-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-10-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-10-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 10 Summary","s":"module-10-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h14","text":"Module 11: Divisional Charts and Their Significations"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Introduction to Vargas and Their Appropriate Use"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 7 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 9."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 11 sorts the positions of the Rashi chart (D1) a second way. A ",{"t":"i","x":"Varga"},", or divisional chart, cuts every sign into parts and gives each part a sign, so it adds no new measurement, only a finer view of positions you already know. The module covers the sixteen divisional charts from D1 to D60, with the ",{"t":"i","x":"Navamsha"}," (D9) and the ",{"t":"i","x":"Dashamsha"}," (D10) in depth. A divisional chart is never read alone."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.1: What Are Vargas?","s":"module-11-lesson-01-what-are-vargas"}," - Cut, count and map; the exceptions."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.2: D2, D3, D4 and D7 (Hora, Drekkana, Chaturthamsha, Saptamsha)","s":"module-11-lesson-02-d2-d3-d4-and-d7"}," - Calculation and cautions for four charts."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.3: The Navamsha (D9) in Depth","s":"module-11-lesson-03-navamsha-d9-in-depth"}," - ",{"t":"i","x":"Vargottama"}," planets (the same sign in D1 and D9); marriage-related themes with cautions."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.4: The Dashamsha (D10) in Depth","s":"module-11-lesson-04-dashamsha-d10-in-depth"}," - The D10 Lagna; career-related themes as a hypothesis."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.5: D12, D16, D20 and D24","s":"module-11-lesson-05-d12-d16-d20-and-d24"}," - Four look-alike start rules, kept apart."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.6: D27, D30, D40, D45 and D60","s":"module-11-lesson-06-d27-d30-d40-d45-and-d60"}," - Advanced charts at the level of awareness."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 11.7: Comparing D1 and D9 Responsibly","s":"module-11-lesson-07-comparing-d1-and-d9-responsibly"}," - Six steps to a qualified conclusion."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Explain what a Varga is, and why it adds no new positions and is not read alone."},{"text":"Calculate the Parashari divisional sign of a Graha or the Lagna in each of the sixteen Vargas, and check it a second way."},{"text":"Find the Navamsha Lagna, recognise a Vargottama Graha and compare D1 and D9 dignity."},{"text":"Locate the D10 Lagna and the D1 factors that tradition reads beside D10."},{"text":"Judge whether a recorded birth time can support a divisional chart, and compare D1 with D9 in six steps."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 11 Practical Exercise","s":"module-11-practical-exercise"}," builds the Navamsha and Dashamsha of a hypothetical horoscope, tests how firm their signs are and compares D9 with D1 on one theme. Use the blank templates in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"}," and the divisional chart worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 5","s":"workbook-05-divisional-dasha-transit-and-integration-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-11-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (30 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-11-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 11 Summary","s":"module-11-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h15","text":"Module 12: Vimshottari Dasha and Its Use"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding Planetary Periods and the Timing Framework"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 7 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 10."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 12 adds time to the chart. A ",{"t":"i","x":"Dasha"}," is a period governed by one planet. The ",{"t":"i","x":"Vimshottari Dasha"}," arranges the time from birth in a cycle of nine main periods, the ",{"t":"i","x":"Mahadashas"},", which total 120 years: Ketu 7, Venus 20, Sun 6, Moon 10, Mars 7, Rahu 18, Jupiter 16, Saturn 19 and Mercury 17. The first period belongs to the lord of the Moon's Nakshatra at birth and the others follow in this order, so a chart does not always begin with Ketu. A Dasha is a timing framework and not a forecast."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 12.1: Dasha Systems and the Vimshottari Framework","s":"module-12-lesson-01-dasha-systems-and-the-vimshottari-framework"}," - The nine lords and why the start varies."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 12.2: The Birth Nakshatra and the Balance of the First Dasha","s":"module-12-lesson-02-birth-nakshatra-and-the-balance-of-the-first-dasha"}," - Calculating the balance and dating the later Mahadashas."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 12.3: Antardasha and Pratyantardasha","s":"module-12-lesson-03-antardasha-and-pratyantardasha"}," - Sub-periods and sub-sub-periods by formula."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 12.4: Interpreting the Dasha Lord","s":"module-12-lesson-04-interpreting-the-dasha-lord"}," - Eight questions to ask of a Dasha lord."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 12.5: Dasha, Transit and Divisional-Chart Integration","s":"module-12-lesson-05-dasha-transit-and-divisional-chart-integration"}," - Joining three tools in one hedged statement."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Name the nine Mahadasha lords in order with their years, show that they total 120, and explain why the first Dasha belongs to the lord of the birth Nakshatra."},{"text":"Calculate the first Mahadasha's balance from a Moon longitude, and date the Mahadashas that follow."},{"text":["Calculate any ",{"t":"i","x":"Antardasha"}," (sub-period) as Mahadasha years x Antardasha years / 120, and extend the rule to the ",{"t":"i","x":"Pratyantardasha"},"."]},{"text":"Assess a Dasha lord with eight questions, and write a hedged statement of the themes a period may bring into focus."},{"text":"Distinguish a Dasha from a transit, and name three settings that move Dasha dates: the birth time, the Ayanamsha and the length of the Dasha year."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 12 Practical Exercise","s":"module-12-practical-exercise"}," works out the balance of a first Mahadasha, reads a dated sequence, tests sub-period lengths, lists the natal factors to examine for a Dasha lord and asks you to find the slip in a worked example. Use the Dasha worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 5","s":"workbook-05-divisional-dasha-transit-and-integration-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-12-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-12-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 12 Summary","s":"module-12-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h16","text":"Module 13: Transits: Gochara"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Understanding Current Planetary Movement in Relation to the Natal Chart"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 6 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 11."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 13 sets the moving sky beside the natal chart, which does not change. ",{"t":"i","x":"Gochara"},", or transit, is the study of where the Grahas stand on a chosen date. You count transits from the natal Moon sign and from the Lagna, meet the traditional tables of favourable houses, check ",{"t":"i","x":"Sade Sati"}," (Saturn's transit through the 12th, 1st and 2nd signs from the natal Moon sign) and use an ephemeris (a table of planetary positions) or a ",{"t":"i","x":"Panchanga"}," (the traditional almanac). Natal, Dasha and transit factors stay in separate columns until the last step."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 13.1: Gochara Basics","s":"module-13-lesson-01-gochara-basics"}," - Counting from the Moon sign and the Lagna."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 13.2: Transit Principles for the Seven Planets","s":"module-13-lesson-02-transit-principles-for-the-seven-planets"}," - Favourable houses and ",{"t":"i","x":"Vedha"}," (obstruction) pairs."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 13.3: Rahu, Ketu and Aspects in Transit","s":"module-13-lesson-03-rahu-ketu-and-aspects-in-transit"}," - The node axis and transit aspects."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 13.4: Saturn and Jupiter Transits, and Sade Sati","s":"module-13-lesson-04-saturn-and-jupiter-transits-and-sade-sati"}," - Sade Sati, ",{"t":"i","x":"Dhaiya"}," (Saturn in the 4th or 8th sign from the Moon sign) and Jupiter, with their qualifications."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 13.5: Reading a Transit Step by Step","s":"module-13-lesson-05-reading-a-transit-step-by-step"}," - Checking an ephemeris; a nine-step method."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Explain Gochara, and tell apart five transit statements, from a planet in a sign to a transit that touches a natal factor."},{"text":"Count a transiting Graha's house from the Moon sign and from the Lagna sign, and say which count the traditional tables use."},{"text":"Look up the favourable houses and Vedha pairs of the seven classical planets, and write a qualified statement instead of a prediction."},{"text":"Place the mean Rahu and Ketu, and check Sade Sati for a Moon sign on a stated date."},{"text":"Follow the nine-step method with an ephemeris or Panchanga, keeping natal, Dasha and transit factors separate."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 13 Practical Exercise","s":"module-13-practical-exercise"}," uses one real date, 26 October 2026 at 12:00 UT, and two hypothetical charts. You rate the transiting Grahas from the natal Moon sign, separate natal, Dasha and transit facts and check Sade Sati for three Moon signs. Use the transit worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 5","s":"workbook-05-divisional-dasha-transit-and-integration-worksheets"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-13-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (25 marks). ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-13-answer-key"},". Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 13 Summary","s":"module-13-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h17","text":"Module 14: Putting It All Together: Integrated Chart Analysis"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"From Individual Principles to a Structured Horoscope Interpretation"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Suggested time:"}," 11 hours. ",{"t":"b","x":"Course outcomes:"}," 12, 13 and 14."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Description."}," Module 14 teaches the order in which to use the tools of Modules 1 to 13: a fifteen-step workflow, a method for weighing evidence and a format for a qualified written reading. You then practise on three public figures: Indira Gandhi, Mahatma Gandhi and Amitabh Bachchan. Their birth data are of unequal quality, so the real lesson is what to trust. No case study links a chart to an event, and astrology is not claimed to explain or cause anyone's achievements."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Topics covered."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 14.1: The Fifteen-Step Chart-Analysis Workflow","s":"module-14-lesson-01-the-fifteen-step-chart-analysis-workflow"}," - Each step with a checklist; one-page worksheet."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 14.2: Weighing Evidence and Writing a Qualified Interpretation","s":"module-14-lesson-02-weighing-evidence-and-writing-an-interpretation"}," - Evidence table; five-part reading."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Case Study 1: Indira Gandhi","s":"module-14-case-study-1-indira-gandhi"}," - A reported birth hour and a probable Lagna."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Case Study 2: Mahatma Gandhi","s":"module-14-case-study-2-mahatma-gandhi"}," - A birth time that no source documents."]},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Case Study 3: Amitabh Bachchan","s":"module-14-case-study-3-amitabh-bachchan"}," - Three layers of trust when accounts differ by hours."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":"Each case study has six parts: biographical context, birth-data verification, chart structure, step-by-step analysis, educational interpretation and a student activity."},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Learning outcomes."}," You will be able to:"]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":"Name the fifteen steps in order and complete the analysis worksheet for a chart, with a source for every finding."},{"text":"Build an evidence table and write a five-part qualified interpretation that names no event, date or advice."},{"text":"Grade a birth time as documented, reported, disputed or unverified, and sort each chart feature as firm or provisional."},{"text":"Test a Yoga, compare D1 with D9 and D10, and read the Dasha and transit without matching any period to an event."},{"text":"Explain the ethical limits of a reading, including consent for another person's chart."}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical activity."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 14 Practical Exercise","s":"module-14-practical-exercise"}," works in stages. Steps 1 to 8 of one hypothetical chart are done for you, you finish steps 9 to 15, and you then read a second chart alone. Use the integration worksheet in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 5","s":"workbook-05-divisional-dasha-transit-and-integration-worksheets"}," and the case-study worksheets in ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 6","s":"workbook-06-case-studies-checklists-and-journal"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Assessment method."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"Quiz and Completion Test","s":"module-14-quiz"},": Part A (20 marks) and Part B (30 marks). The ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"module-14-answer-key"}," also holds the case-study answer guides. Checklist in the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 14 Summary","s":"module-14-summary"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h18","text":"Beyond the Modules"},
{"type":"para","text":"Three kinds of work sit beside the modules. Any pass marks for them are recommended settings that the LMS administrator may adjust."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Final Assessment."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Final Assessment","s":"assessment-final-assessment"}," has 50 questions across the whole course and 100 marks. Its ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Key","s":"assessment-final-assessment-answer-key"}," is separate."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical Chart-Reading Examination."}," The ",{"t":"a","x":"Practical Chart-Reading Examination","s":"assessment-practical-chart-reading-examination"}," has three exercises of rising difficulty: identification, analysis and integration. A rubric rates accuracy, method, reasoning, terminology and appropriate qualifications. The ",{"t":"a","x":"Answer Guide","s":"assessment-practical-examination-answer-guide"}," gives model answers."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Student Workbook."}," The workbook has six pages for digital or printed use."]}]},
{"type":"table","caption":"The six Student Workbook pages","cols":3,"header":["Workbook page","Contents","Used with"],"rows":[[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 1","s":"workbook-01-goals-and-terminology"}],"Goals and terminology","The whole course"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"}],"Chart templates; house, planet and sign worksheets","Modules 1 to 4 and 11"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"}],"Dignity, classification, Yoga and Ascendant worksheets","Modules 5 to 9"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 4","s":"workbook-04-sambandha-and-nakshatra-worksheets"}],"Sambandha and Nakshatra worksheets","Module 10"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 5","s":"workbook-05-divisional-dasha-transit-and-integration-worksheets"}],"Divisional chart, Dasha, transit and integration worksheets","Modules 11 to 14"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 6","s":"workbook-06-case-studies-checklists-and-journal"}],"Case-study worksheets, self-assessment, journal and revision notes","Module 14 and final revision"]]},
{"type":"para","text":["The ",{"t":"a","x":"Assessment Framework and Completion Criteria","s":"assessment-framework-and-completion-criteria"}," page explains how the quizzes, tests, final assessment and examination fit together, and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Completion","s":"course-completion"}," page gives the closing checklist. For help along the way, use the ",{"t":"a","x":"Frequently Asked Questions","s":"faq-beginner-questions"},", the ",{"t":"a","x":"Glossary of Sanskrit and Technical Terms","s":"glossary-sanskrit-and-technical-terms"}," and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Reference Library","s":"reference-library"},"."]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"REMEMBER","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Understand the Principles. Analyse the Relationships. Build the Foundation. Every module follows one sequence: Understand, Observe, Analyse, Establish Relationships, Verify, Interpret."}]}
]},
"course-home-instructor":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"Welcome to the Course Team"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Basics of Vedic Astrology: A Complete Beginner's Course"}," is presented by ASTROSUTRAS by Anoopam Shukla. The course team has two Chief Course Instructors, Anoopam Shukla and Upendra Singh Bhadoriya, and two Instructors and Evaluators, Kaushik Mitra and Shweta Vijayvarrgiya. The course also names its lineage, Mata Saraswati. This page introduces the team and the lineage, gives the professional profile that was supplied for Anoopam Shukla, and explains the ASTROSUTRAS approach on which every lesson is built."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"The Course Team"},
{"type":"para","text":"The course team has two roles, Chief Course Instructor and Instructor and Evaluator. Each member is listed below under the role that was supplied."},
{"type":"people","title":"Meet the course team","columns":2,"people":[{"name":"Anoopam Shukla","role":"Chief Course Instructor","photo":"assets/team/anoopam-shukla.jpg","alt":"Photograph of Anoopam Shukla"},{"name":"Upendra Singh Bhadoriya","role":"Chief Course Instructor","photo":"assets/team/upendra-singh-bhadoriya.jpg","alt":"Photograph of Upendra Singh Bhadoriya"},{"name":"Kaushik Mitra","role":"Instructor and Evaluator","photo":"assets/team/kaushik-mitra.jpg","alt":"Photograph of Kaushik Mitra"},{"name":"Shweta Vijayvarrgiya","role":"Instructor and Evaluator","photo":"assets/team/shweta-vijayvarrgiya.jpg","alt":"Photograph of Shweta Vijayvarrgiya"}]},
{"type":"table","caption":"The course team","cols":2,"header":["Role","Name"],"rows":[["Chief Course Instructor","Anoopam Shukla"],["Chief Course Instructor","Upendra Singh Bhadoriya"],["Instructor and Evaluator","Kaushik Mitra"],["Instructor and Evaluator","Shweta Vijayvarrgiya"]]},
{"type":"para","text":"The roles and names are given exactly as the course owner supplied them. The sections that follow give a profile for Anoopam Shukla only, using only the facts supplied for him. This page adds no biography, qualification or experience for the other members of the team, and it does not describe the work of the Instructors and Evaluators beyond the name of the role."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"Lineage"},
{"type":"para","text":"The lineage of the course is Mata Saraswati. Mata Saraswati is traditionally honoured in India as the goddess of knowledge, learning and the arts."},
{"type":"para","text":"The course names this lineage as supplied. It does not state or imply any line of teaching succession, any initiation or any link with an institution. The lessons themselves rest on stated rules, conditions and worked examples that you can check for yourself, as the approach described below explains."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"Profile of Anoopam Shukla"},
{"type":"para","text":["Anoopam Shukla is one of the two Chief Course Instructors of ",{"t":"i","x":"Basics of Vedic Astrology: A Complete Beginner's Course"},", which is presented by ASTROSUTRAS by Anoopam Shukla. He is a Marine Engineer and a Market Expert. He is also a Vedic Astrologer, Researcher, Author and Teacher. His engagement with astrology education, research and the development of structured predictive frameworks is long-standing, and he has taught both offline and online."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"Professional Background of Anoopam Shukla"},
{"type":"para","text":"The experience of Anoopam Shukla in astrology includes the following areas."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Vedic Astrology."}," The subject of this course, taught here from its foundations."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Jaimini principles."}," A separate system of Jyotisha that works mainly from signs rather than from planets."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Nadi Jyotish."}," A tradition associated with Tamil Nadu and with palm-leaf manuscripts."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Tajik Astrology."}," A Perso-Arabic-influenced system of Indian astrology, known for its annual charts."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Financial Astrology."}," The use of astrological ideas in the study of markets."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":"These names follow the profile of Anoopam Shukla as supplied. Elsewhere the course writes Jyotisha and Tajika. This foundation course teaches the Parashari approach to Vedic astrology only. It names the Jaimini, Tajika and Nadi systems so that you can recognise them, and it does not teach them. It does not teach Financial Astrology, and nothing in it is financial advice. Like Vedic astrology itself, none of these areas is presented here as scientifically established."},
{"type":"para","text":"The wider work of Anoopam Shukla includes structured predictive frameworks. This course has a narrower purpose: it teaches interpretation. It describes themes and tendencies, tests them against stated rules and does not forecast events."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h6","text":"The ASTROSUTRAS Approach"},
{"type":"para","text":"The ASTROSUTRAS approach combines four things: traditional knowledge, systematic observation, research and practical application. It is the approach of the course as a whole, and each of the four has a visible place on its pages."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Traditional knowledge."}," Classical conventions are taught as conventions. Classical texts are named only as general sources, with no verse quotations and no chapter or verse numbers. Where schools differ, a SCHOOLS box says so."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Systematic observation."}," You record exactly what a chart shows before you judge it. Positions come first, and interpretation comes second."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Research."}," Every rule is tested against its conditions and exceptions. Numbers are checked with a calculator or a stated table, and a rule that rests on a modern convention is labelled as one."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practical application."}," You work through worked examples, practical exercises and three case studies, so that each idea is used and not only read."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":["The course puts these four into practice through its six-step reading method: Understand, Observe, Analyse, Establish Relationships, Verify, Interpret. Understand is where traditional knowledge enters. Observe is systematic observation. Analyse and Establish Relationships break a chart into factors and link them. Verify is the research habit: check the data, the rule and the exceptions. Interpret is practical application, ending in a qualified statement. The sequence is a teaching device of this course and not a classical procedure. You can read more about it in ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.1","s":"module-01-lesson-01-what-is-a-kundali"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h7","text":"What This Means for You as a Learner"},
{"type":"para","text":"You can expect a clear path, with reasons given for every rule and not only the rule itself. You can expect honesty about the places where traditions disagree, and wording that is careful, never alarming. You can expect to practise, because the method only becomes yours when you use it on charts."},
{"type":"para","text":["You should also expect a foundation and not a finished craft. The areas of experience listed above reach well beyond a beginner course. This course gives you a precise vocabulary and a habit of verification on which later study can build, and this course presents it as a first step and not as the whole of the subject. ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Level and Intended Audience","s":"course-home-course-level"}," explains what the course does and does not make you, and ",{"t":"a","x":"How to Use This Course","s":"course-home-how-to-use"}," shows how to get the most from it."]}
]},
"course-home-how-to-use":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"A Self-Paced Course"},
{"type":"para","text":["This course is self-paced (see ",{"t":"a","x":"About the Course","s":"course-home-about-the-course"}," for its scope). You choose when to study, and no live class is assumed. Everything you need to check your own work is on the pages: the Answer Key of each module gives the answers, a short explanation of each and the lesson to revisit."]},
{"type":"para","text":"The 14 modules are cumulative. Each one assumes the ones before it, so how you study matters more than how fast you go."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"Ten Recommended Practices"},
{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Study the modules in order."}," Module 9 (the twelve Ascendants) uses the house groups of Module 6 and the ",{"t":"i","x":"Yogas"}," (named planetary combinations) of Modules 7 and 8. A skipped module leaves a gap that later pages assume you have filled."],"ordered":true,"n":1},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Read the lesson before the assessment."}," A quiz taken before the lesson mostly measures guessing."],"ordered":true,"n":2},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Keep a personal astrology notebook."}," Writing by hand slows you down enough to notice details. A layout is suggested below."],"ordered":true,"n":3},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Keep a glossary of Sanskrit and technical terms."}," Copy each new term with its meaning in your own words. The course ",{"t":"a","x":"Glossary","s":"glossary-sanskrit-and-technical-terms"}," is for checking, not for replacing your list."],"ordered":true,"n":4},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Practise houses, signs, planets and lordships."}," Every later step counts a house or names a lord. Start a session with a five-minute drill, such as naming the sign and lord of each house for a ",{"t":"i","x":"Lagna"}," (rising sign) chosen at random."],"ordered":true,"n":5},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Complete the workbook exercises after each module."}," The lessons explain; the worksheets make you work from memory."],"ordered":true,"n":6},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Attempt each quiz without looking at the answers."}," Trying to recall before you check shows what you really know."],"ordered":true,"n":7},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Revisit difficult concepts before you move on."}," The Answer Key names a lesson for each question. A small gap carried forward grows."],"ordered":true,"n":8},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Use the case studies for integrated reasoning."}," The three case studies of Module 14 are where you combine everything. Do them after Modules 1 to 13."],"ordered":true,"n":9},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Treat every interpretation as a hypothesis to examine."}," A hypothesis names what you looked at, says what it may suggest and states what would count against it. A prediction names an outcome and leaves no room to be wrong."],"ordered":true,"n":10}]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"RULE","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Learn first, test second. Read the lesson, redo its Worked Example on paper, and only then attempt the practical exercise and the quiz."}]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"EXAMPLE","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"A hypothesis: \"Venus is traditionally associated with relationships and the 7th house with partnership, so Venus in the 7th may bring partnership themes into focus.\" A prediction would say \"this person will marry early\". The course teaches the first form and not the second."}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"How a Module Is Organised"},
{"type":"para","text":"Every module has the same page set, so you always know where you are."},
{"type":"table","caption":"The pages of a module","cols":3,"header":["Page","What it is for","When to use it"],"rows":[["Introduction","Objectives, links to what you need first, a map of the lessons and a suggested time","Before the first lesson"],["Lessons","The teaching, from What You Will Learn to Pause and Reflect","In order, with a pencil"],["Practical Exercise","A guided worked example, then exercises on hypothetical charts","After the last lesson"],["Quiz and Completion Test","Part A is a quiz of mixed question types. Part B tests reasoning","With the Answer Key closed"],["Answer Key","Answers, brief explanations, the lesson to revisit and the answers to the practical","After you finish the quiz"],["Summary","Principles, takeaways, terms and a module checklist","To close the module, and before the final assessment"]]},
{"type":"para","text":["The Pause and Reflect questions have no printed answers. Write your own in the notebook. ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 9","s":"module-09-introduction"}," also holds twelve Ascendant chapters, one for each rising sign, and ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 14","s":"module-14-introduction"}," holds the three case studies."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"The Boxes in the Lessons"},
{"type":"table","caption":"The eight kinds of box","cols":4,"header":["Course name","On screen","In print","What it tells you, and what to do"],"rows":[["NOTE","Note","NOTE","An extra explanation. Read it as context"],["TIP","Practical Tip","TIP","A practical reading tip. Try it in your next exercise"],["RULE","Important Rule","RULE","A principle to remember. Copy it into your notebook"],["WARNING","Common Error","WARNING","A common error or misconception. Check your work against it"],["EXAMPLE","Example","EXAMPLE","A short worked mini-example. Redo it on paper"],["REMEMBER","Remember","REMEMBER","A mnemonic or a rule to memorise. Learn it by heart"],["SCHOOLS","School Variation","SCHOOLS DIFFER","A place where traditions or texts differ. Learn the course default and note the variants"],["ACTIVITY","Activity","ACTIVITY","A hands-on task, always with a text alternative. Do the task or its alternative"]]},
{"type":"para","text":"Every box carries a printed label, so you never need colour to tell them apart. This course calls a box by its first column, for example a SCHOOLS box."},
{"type":"callout","variant":"SCHOOLS","lines":[{"k":"p","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Jyotisha"},", the Sanskrit name for Vedic astrology, is not one rulebook. A SCHOOLS box states the course default first and then names the variants that other texts, teachers or software use. It does this to be honest about the field. It does not suggest that the default is the only correct method or that a variant is wrong. In exercises and quizzes, use the course default unless a question says otherwise."]}]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"REMEMBER","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Understand, Observe, Analyse, Establish Relationships, Verify, Interpret. Lessons follow this order where it fits, and Interpret comes last. It is this course's own teaching sequence, not a classical procedure."}]},
{"type":"para","text":["The defaults and assumptions behind every lesson are listed on ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Conventions and Instructional Assumptions","s":"course-home-conventions-and-assumptions"},"."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"A Suggested Weekly Routine"},
{"type":"para","text":"The course suggests about 100 hours of study over 16 weeks, a weekly routine of about 6 to 7 hours. This is a suggestion, not a requirement. The final assessment, the practical examination and the workbook fit inside the 16 weeks and are not added on top of that figure."},
{"type":"table","caption":"One week, session by session","cols":3,"header":["Session","Time","What to do"],"rows":[["1","90 minutes","Read the module introduction and the first lessons. Add new terms to your glossary"],["2","90 minutes","Finish the lessons. Redo each Worked Example on paper. Write your Pause and Reflect answers"],["3","90 minutes","Do the practical exercise: the Guided Worked Practical first, then the exercises"],["4","60 minutes","Complete the workbook worksheets for the module"],["5","60 minutes","Take the quiz and the completion test closed-book. Check the Answer Key, revisit weak lessons, read the Summary and tick its checklist"],["Catch-up","up to 30 minutes","Keep it free for a revisit or an unfinished task"]]},
{"type":"para","text":["Over the 16 weeks the plan runs like this. The ",{"t":"a","x":"Syllabus","s":"course-home-syllabus"}," gives the same plan with hours for each week."]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Week 1:"}," the Course Home pages and Module 1."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Weeks 2 to 8:"}," one module a week, Modules 2 to 8."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Weeks 9 and 10:"}," Module 9, the longest module."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Weeks 11 to 15:"}," one module a week, Modules 10 to 14."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Week 16:"}," the final assessment, the practical examination and the Course Completion page."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":["Worksheet 1.2 of ",{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 1","s":"workbook-01-goals-and-terminology"}," turns this outline into a dated plan."]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"TIP","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"The suggested time of a module varies, from about five hours for Module 6 to more than fifteen for Module 9. In a heavy week, spread the sessions over more days. If you fall behind, move the whole plan later and keep the order."}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h6","text":"Your Astrology Notebook"},
{"type":"para","text":"A notebook, kept by hand or on a screen, is where the course becomes yours. Divide it into five parts."},
{"type":"table","caption":"A notebook plan","cols":2,"header":["Part","What goes in it"],"rows":[["1. Glossary","The term, its meaning in your own words and the lesson where you met it"],["2. Chart pages","One page for each chart you draw, laid out as shown below"],["3. Lesson notes","Every RULE and REMEMBER box copied by hand, and each Worked Example redone"],["4. Error log","The date, the mistake, its cause (a memory gap, a counting slip or two terms mixed up) and the fix"],["5. Hypothesis log","Facts first, then a hedged hypothesis and what would count against it, in the form of Section 22 of the Student Workbook"]]},
{"type":"text","title":"One chart page in the notebook","lines":["+----------------------------------------------------+","| Title, and the label: hypothetical / own / agreed  |","| Settings: sidereal, Lahiri, whole sign, mean node, |","| time zone                                          |","+--------------------------+-------------------------+","| Hand-drawn chart         | Table of houses:        |","| (North or South style)   | sign, lord, occupants   |","+--------------------------+-------------------------+","| FACTS (what the chart shows)                       |","| HYPOTHESES (hedged) | for | against                |","| STILL UNVERIFIED                                   |","+----------------------------------------------------+"]},
{"type":"para","text":["The page has three bands. The top band names the chart, says whether it is hypothetical, your own or drawn with its owner's permission, and records the five chart settings of ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.3","s":"module-01-lesson-03-birth-data-and-chart-calculation"},". The middle band holds your drawing and a table of houses, signs, lords and occupants. The bottom band keeps facts apart from judgement."]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"ACTIVITY","lines":[{"k":"p","text":["Rule the five parts in your notebook, then list the first ten terms of ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.1","s":"module-01-lesson-01-what-is-a-kundali"}," in Part 1. Text alternative: on paper, write the numbers 1 to 5 on the first five pages with the part names beside them, then list ten terms on the first page."]}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h7","text":"Using the Student Workbook"},
{"type":"para","text":"The Student Workbook has 23 worksheet sections on six pages. Each worksheet has a learning objective, short instructions, a worked example where one helps, space for your answers and a self-check."},
{"type":"table","caption":"When to use each workbook page","cols":3,"header":["Workbook page","Sections","Use it with"],"rows":[[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 1","s":"workbook-01-goals-and-terminology"}],"1 and 2","The start of the course"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 2","s":"workbook-02-templates-and-reference-worksheets"}],"3 to 7","Modules 1 to 4"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 3","s":"workbook-03-dignity-classification-and-yoga-worksheets"}],"8 to 11","Modules 5 to 9"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 4","s":"workbook-04-sambandha-and-nakshatra-worksheets"}],"12 to 15","Module 10"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 5","s":"workbook-05-divisional-dasha-transit-and-integration-worksheets"}],"16 to 19","Modules 11 to 14"],[[{"t":"a","x":"Student Workbook 6","s":"workbook-06-case-studies-checklists-and-journal"}],"20 to 23","Module 14 onward"]]},
{"type":"para","text":["Work each worksheet in five steps. ",{"t":"b","x":"Recall"}," what you can from memory, in pencil. ",{"t":"b","x":"Look up"}," only the empty cells. ",{"t":"b","x":"Check"}," against the self-check. ",{"t":"b","x":"Diagnose"}," each error. ",{"t":"b","x":"Repeat"}," the worksheet on a clean copy a day or more later."]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"WARNING","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Do not read a worksheet's key before you have filled the blanks. A key you have already seen cannot test your memory."}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h8","text":"Progress-Completion Checklist"},
{"type":"para","text":["Tick each item only when it is true. The pass levels below, 60 percent on a quiz and 60 marks out of 100 on each examination, are recommended settings that your LMS administrator may adjust; see the ",{"t":"a","x":"Assessment Framework","s":"assessment-framework-and-completion-criteria"},"."]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"For every module"}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"checklist":true,"id":"ck1","items":[{"text":"I read each lesson, redid its Worked Example and wrote my own Pause and Reflect answers.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.1"},{"text":"I completed the practical exercise and compared it with the Answer Key.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.2"},{"text":"I took the quiz and the completion test without the Answer Key and scored at least 60 percent in each part.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.3"},{"text":"I revisited the lessons named for the questions I missed.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.4"},{"text":"I completed the workbook worksheets and updated my glossary.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.5"},{"text":"I read the Summary and ticked its checklist.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.6"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"By module: I can ..."}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"checklist":true,"id":"ck2","items":[{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 1","s":"module-01-introduction"},": draw and read one chart in the North and the South Indian style."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.1"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 2","s":"module-02-introduction"},": name the twelve houses with their core themes and turn an everyday question into a house."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.2"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 3","s":"module-03-introduction"},": describe each of the nine ",{"t":"i","x":"Grahas"}," (planets) and tell its natural nature from its functional role."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.3"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 4","s":"module-04-introduction"},": find the sign and lord of any house for any Lagna."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.4"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 5","s":"module-05-introduction"},": classify a planet's dignity at a given degree and explain why dignity is not strength."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.5"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 6","s":"module-06-introduction"},": list every group of one house."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.6"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 7","s":"module-07-introduction"},": test a chart for the good Yogas taught and give a verdict of present, verify or absent."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.7"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 8","s":"module-08-introduction"},": weigh a challenging combination in its full context without fear-based wording."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.8"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 9","s":"module-09-introduction"},": give the house lords, functional roles and any ",{"t":"i","x":"Yogakaraka"}," for any Lagna."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.9"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 10","s":"module-10-introduction"},": trace the four kinds of ",{"t":"i","x":"Sambandha"}," in a chart and state what I can and cannot say."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.10"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 11","s":"module-11-introduction"},": find a divisional sign in the sixteen Vargas and compare D1 with D9 responsibly."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.11"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 12","s":"module-12-introduction"},": calculate the balance of the first ",{"t":"i","x":"Mahadasha"}," and name the running Mahadasha and ",{"t":"i","x":"Antardasha"},"."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.12"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 13","s":"module-13-introduction"},": count a transit from the Moon sign and the Lagna and check ",{"t":"i","x":"Sade Sati"}," for a stated date."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.13"},{"text":[{"t":"a","x":"Module 14","s":"module-14-introduction"},": complete the fifteen-step worksheet and write a five-part qualified interpretation."],"checked":false,"id":"ck2.14"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Final stage"}]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"checklist":true,"id":"ck3","items":[{"text":["I completed the ",{"t":"a","x":"Final Assessment","s":"assessment-final-assessment"}," and scored at least 60 marks out of 100."],"checked":false,"id":"ck3.1"},{"text":["I completed the ",{"t":"a","x":"Practical Chart-Reading Examination","s":"assessment-practical-chart-reading-examination"},", with its identification, analysis and integration exercises, and scored at least 60 marks out of 100."],"checked":false,"id":"ck3.2"},{"text":["I read ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Completion","s":"course-completion"}," and wrote down what I can and cannot do with what I have learned."],"checked":false,"id":"ck3.3"}]}
]},
"course-home-prerequisites":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"You Need No Astrology Knowledge"},
{"type":"para","text":"This is a beginner course. It assumes no earlier study of astrology, no Sanskrit and no mathematics beyond simple arithmetic. If you have met some terms in books or videos, you may keep what you know, but the course defines every term at its first use and builds each idea from the start. What helps is a handful of ordinary skills, and you can build each of them as you go."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"Six Helpful Skills, with a Self-Test"},
{"type":"table","caption":"Three signs, for the first self-test","cols":3,"header":["Sign number","English name","Sanskrit name"],"rows":[["1","Aries","Mesha"],["2","Taurus","Vrishabha"],["3","Gemini","Mithuna"]]},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Read a simple table."}," Find the row, then the column. ",{"t":"i","x":"Self-test:"}," what is the Sanskrit name of sign 3 in the table above?"]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Follow a twelve-house diagram."}," A chart is a diagram of twelve compartments. ",{"t":"i","x":"Self-test:"}," in the North Indian frame in the next section, how many compartments are diamonds and how many are triangles?"]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Know a few astronomy words."}," The mini-glossary below lists them. ",{"t":"i","x":"Self-test:"}," before you read it, write one sentence on what the ecliptic is, then compare."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Follow step-by-step instructions."}," Most methods in the course are short lists of steps. ",{"t":"i","x":"Self-test:"}," start at 10, add 5, and if the answer is above 12, subtract 12. What do you get?"]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Do simple arithmetic."}," You will subtract times and divide with a remainder. ",{"t":"i","x":"Self-test:"}," subtract 5 hours 30 minutes from 12:00. Then divide 100 by 30 and give the whole number and the remainder."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Keep notes and do exercises."}," ",{"t":"i","x":"Self-test:"}," is a notebook ready, with today's date and the course name on its first page?"]}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"b","x":"Answers."}," Table: Mithuna. Diagram: twelve compartments, four diamonds and eight triangles. Words: your sentence should match the glossary. Steps: 3. Arithmetic: 06:30, then 3 with a remainder of 10. Notes: a plain yes or no."]},
{"type":"para","text":["If you missed an answer, do not worry: ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 1","s":"module-01-introduction"}," practises each skill step by step."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"A First Look at a Chart"},
{"type":"chart","title":"The two chart frames, blank (preview)","note":"Preview only. Lessons 1.6 and 1.7 teach both frames.","style":"both","blank":true,"answerSafe":false,"signs":[{"no":1,"name":"Aries","items":[]},{"no":2,"name":"Taurus","items":[]},{"no":3,"name":"Gemini","items":[]},{"no":4,"name":"Cancer","items":[]},{"no":5,"name":"Leo","items":[]},{"no":6,"name":"Virgo","items":[]},{"no":7,"name":"Libra","items":[]},{"no":8,"name":"Scorpio","items":[]},{"no":9,"name":"Sagittarius","items":[]},{"no":10,"name":"Capricorn","items":[]},{"no":11,"name":"Aquarius","items":[]},{"no":12,"name":"Pisces","items":[]}],"alt":{"north":"Blank North Indian style chart template titled \"The two chart frames, blank (preview)\". It is drawn as a square holding twelve compartments: four diamonds (top, left, bottom, right) and eight corner triangles. The houses are fixed in position and numbered anticlockwise starting from the top diamond, which is house 1 (the Lagna house). The template is empty; each compartment shows only its house number. House 1 (top diamond). House 2 (top-left triangle touching the top edge). House 3 (left-top triangle touching the left edge). House 4 (left diamond). House 5 (left-bottom triangle touching the left edge). House 6 (bottom-left triangle touching the bottom edge). House 7 (bottom diamond). House 8 (bottom-right triangle touching the bottom edge). House 9 (right-bottom triangle touching the right edge). House 10 (right diamond). House 11 (right-top triangle touching the right edge). House 12 (top-right triangle touching the top edge).","south":"Blank South Indian style chart template titled \"The two chart frames, blank (preview)\". It is a four-by-four grid whose centre two-by-two block is empty, leaving twelve sign cells around the edge. The signs are fixed in their cells: along the top row from the left Pisces, Aries, Taurus, Gemini; down the right column Cancer, Leo; along the bottom row from the right Virgo, Libra, Scorpio, Sagittarius; up the left column Capricorn, Aquarius. The signs therefore run clockwise from Aries. The template is empty; each cell shows only its sign name."},"data":null},
{"type":"para","text":["In the North Indian frame the twelve houses keep fixed places: house 1 is the top diamond, and the numbers run anticlockwise around the frame. In the South Indian grid the twelve signs keep fixed cells: Aries is the second cell of the top row, and the signs run clockwise. You do not need to read either one yet. ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.6","s":"module-01-lesson-06-reading-the-north-indian-chart"}," and ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.7","s":"module-01-lesson-07-reading-the-south-indian-chart"}," teach them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"A Mini-Glossary of Astronomy Words"},
{"type":"para","text":["These plain-language meanings are enough to start. ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.2","s":"module-01-lesson-02-sidereal-zodiac-and-ayanamsha"}," and ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.3","s":"module-01-lesson-03-birth-data-and-chart-calculation"}," teach the ideas again, with numbers."]},
{"type":"table","caption":"Astronomy words in plain language","cols":3,"header":["Word","Plain meaning","Taught in"],"rows":[["Sun","The star at the centre of our solar system. Seen from the Earth, it appears to travel once around the zodiac in a year","Lesson 1.2"],["Moon","The Earth's natural satellite. It moves about 13 degrees a day and changes sign about every two and a quarter days","Lesson 1.3"],["Planet","In astronomy, a large body that orbits the Sun. The course also calls each of the nine Grahas a planet, including the Sun, the Moon, Rahu and Ketu","Lesson 1.4"],["Zodiac","The belt of sky around the ecliptic, divided into twelve signs of 30 degrees each","Lesson 1.2"],["Ecliptic","The Sun's apparent yearly path against the background stars. The Moon and planets stay close to it","Lesson 1.2"],["Retrograde","A planet's apparent backward movement against the stars, seen from the Earth. It is an effect of viewpoint. The Sun and Moon do not show it","Lesson 5.4"],["Eclipse","Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, or the Moon's on Earth, as the Sun, Earth and Moon line up. It happens only near the Moon's nodes, Rahu and Ketu","Lesson 3.6"],["Longitude and latitude","On the Earth, the angle east or west of Greenwich and the angle north or south of the equator. Along the zodiac, a position from 0 to 360 degrees","Lessons 1.2 and 1.3"],["Time zone","A region that keeps one standard clock time, written as an offset from UTC. Indian Standard Time is UTC+05:30","Lesson 1.3"]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"How Sanskrit Is Introduced"},
{"type":"para","text":["Sanskrit comes in gradually. A new term appears in italics at its first use on a page, with its English meaning beside it, and is used consistently afterwards. Signs and planets are written in English in running text, with the Sanskrit name as a companion at first use, as in Aries (",{"t":"i","x":"Mesha"},"). Every lesson has a Key Terms table, and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Glossary","s":"glossary-sanskrit-and-technical-terms"}," collects the terms of the whole course."]},
{"type":"para","text":["The course writes Sanskrit in plain English letters with no accents or special marks, and one spelling for each term. When another spelling is common in books or software, the course mentions it once. ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Conventions and Instructional Assumptions","s":"course-home-conventions-and-assumptions"}," lists the spelling choices."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h6","text":"What You Will Need"},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"A notebook and pencil."}," ",{"t":"a","x":"How to Use This Course","s":"course-home-how-to-use"}," suggests a layout."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"The Student Workbook."}," It holds the blank North and South Indian templates and the worksheets."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"A basic calculator."}]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Optionally, a free chart-calculation program."}," It is useful for extra practice. Choose one in which you can set the zodiac, the ",{"t":"i","x":"Ayanamsha"}," (the offset between the Western and Vedic zodiacs), the house system, the node type and the time zone. The course names no product and endorses none."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":"The course settings are the sidereal zodiac, the Lahiri Ayanamsha, whole-sign houses, the mean node and the time-zone offset in force at the birthplace on the birth date. To test a program, enter 26 October 2026 at 12:00 UT, at any place. With these settings the Sun should stand close to Libra 8°51'."},
{"type":"callout","variant":"NOTE","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Every number in a worked example comes from a stated tool or table, so you can follow the course with the charts and tables printed in it, with or without a program."}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h7","text":"Privacy"},
{"type":"callout","variant":"WARNING","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Birth data is personal. Use another person's birth data only with their permission, and for a child ask a parent or guardian. A date, time and place with a name can identify someone, so do not post them in public. The course never needs real data: its examples are hypothetical charts, apart from the three public-figure case studies of Module 14, which state their sources."}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h8","text":"Technical Set-Up"},
{"type":"para","text":"Any recent computer, tablet or phone with an up-to-date web browser will do. The pages are built to read on a phone: tables have at most six columns, every chart comes with a written description, and no lesson depends on an external image or on colour alone. Each interactive activity has a text alternative that you can do with paper and pencil."}
]},
"course-home-course-level":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"Level: Beginner and Foundation"},
{"type":"para","text":"This is a Beginner / Foundation course. It assumes no knowledge of astrology. It teaches the vocabulary, the structure of a chart and a repeatable method for reading it with care. It is a first step into Vedic astrology, not the whole of the subject."},
{"type":"para","text":["The modules are cumulative. Module 1 starts from the meaning of a ",{"t":"i","x":"Kundali"},", and each later module uses the tools of the one before. A suggested pace is about 100 hours over 16 weeks, which is a weekly routine of 6 to 7 hours. That is a suggestion and not a requirement."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"Who This Course Is For"},
{"type":"para","text":"Five kinds of learner will find a place here."},
{"type":"table","caption":"The five audiences, what each gains and where each should start","cols":3,"header":["Learner","What you gain","Where to start"],"rows":[["Complete beginners","A first map of the whole subject, with every term explained when it first appears",["Read the ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Roadmap","s":"course-home-roadmap"},", then open the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 1 Introduction","s":"module-01-introduction"}]],["Students who have just begun Vedic astrology","A tidy base under what you have picked up, and a check of your settings and vocabulary",["Read ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Conventions and Instructional Assumptions","s":"course-home-conventions-and-assumptions"},", then do Module 1 even if it feels familiar"]],["Enthusiasts who want a systematic foundation","A complete, ordered path with the reason behind each rule",["Read the ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Roadmap","s":"course-home-roadmap"}," and follow the modules in order"]],["Learners who know isolated concepts but lack a structured method","A repeatable sequence that connects what you already know",["Move faster through familiar lessons, but do every quiz so that gaps show, and give time to ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 7.7","s":"module-07-lesson-07-verifying-a-yoga-step-by-step"},", ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 10.6","s":"module-10-lesson-06-integrating-relationships"}," and ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 14.1","s":"module-14-lesson-01-the-fifteen-step-chart-analysis-workflow"}]],["Aspiring students preparing for intermediate studies","Fluent vocabulary and calculation habits on which later study is likely to build",["Study every module, then plan your next steps with the ",{"t":"a","x":"Reference Library","s":"reference-library"}]]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"What the Course Gives You and What It Does Not"},
{"type":"para","text":"After the course you will have a foundation. You will know the vocabulary, you will be able to calculate and check the main numbers of a chart, and you will have a method for reading a chart and stating a qualified conclusion. You will have practised on hypothetical charts and on three public-figure case studies."},
{"type":"callout","variant":"WARNING","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"This course does not make you an expert predictive astrologer. No course of this length can. Skilled reading is a craft."}]},
{"type":"para","text":["That craft grows with practice on many charts, with a teacher or mentor who can review your reasoning and correct it, and with further study of the areas this course only names. There is no fixed number of charts or hours that gives skill, and this course gives only the rough planning guide in answer 29 of the ",{"t":"a","x":"Frequently Asked Questions","s":"faq-beginner-questions"},". Treat completion as the end of the foundation and the start of practice."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"What This Course Is Not"},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Not a prediction service."}," It teaches how to interpret themes and tendencies. It predicts no events, gives no dates for events and offers no personal chart readings."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Not professional advice."}," Nothing in it is medical, legal or financial advice."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Not a scientific claim."}," It presents Vedic astrology (Jyotisha) as a traditional interpretive framework, not as a scientifically established method."]},{"text":[{"t":"b","x":"Not a complete survey."}," The following are only named, and are not taught: horoscope matching (the scored ",{"t":"i","x":"Kuta"}," factors of traditional marriage matching), ",{"t":"i","x":"Muhurta"}," (choosing a favourable time), ",{"t":"i","x":"Prashna"}," (answering a question from a chart cast for the moment of asking), ",{"t":"i","x":"Ashtakavarga"}," (a point-scoring technique), the Jaimini, Tajika and Nadi systems, and Dasha systems other than Vimshottari."]}]},
{"type":"para","text":["Naming these subjects lets you recognise the words when you meet them. The ",{"t":"a","x":"Reference Library","s":"reference-library"}," and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Frequently Asked Questions","s":"faq-beginner-questions"}," point to ways to continue."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"Is This Course Right for Me?"},
{"type":"para","text":"Tick each statement that is true for you."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"checklist":true,"id":"ck1","items":[{"text":"I want to learn how a horoscope is built and read, and I am happy to start from first principles.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.1"},{"text":"I can follow step-by-step instructions and do simple arithmetic, such as dividing by 30 and counting around a ring of twelve.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.2"},{"text":"I am willing to treat every reading as a hypothesis to examine and not as a certain prediction.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.3"},{"text":"I accept that Sanskrit terms will be introduced gradually, each with its English meaning.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.4"},{"text":"I can set aside about 6 to 7 hours a week for about 16 weeks, or I will adjust the pace to suit me.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.5"},{"text":"I will use another person's birth data only with their permission.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.6"},{"text":"I understand that I will finish with a foundation and not with expert predictive skill.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.7"}]},
{"type":"para","text":["If you ticked most of the boxes, read the ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Roadmap","s":"course-home-roadmap"}," and ",{"t":"a","x":"Course Conventions and Instructional Assumptions","s":"course-home-conventions-and-assumptions"},", and then open the ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 1 Introduction","s":"module-01-introduction"},". If you were hoping for event predictions or a quick expert qualification, this course is probably not the right fit, and it is better to know that before you begin."]}
]},
"course-home-roadmap":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"The Shape of the Course"},
{"type":"para","text":"The course climbs in twelve stages, and each stage rests on the one before it. Its approach moves from conceptual understanding to systematic analysis, then to practical application and integrated chart interpretation. The diagram shows the order. The table below it links each stage to its modules."},
{"type":"flow","dir":"TD","title":"The twelve stages of the course","nodes":[{"id":"N1","label":"Foundation"},{"id":"N2","label":"Horoscope Structure"},{"id":"N3","label":"Core Components"},{"id":"N4","label":"Planetary Strength"},{"id":"N5","label":"Yogas"},{"id":"N6","label":"Ascendant Analysis"},{"id":"N7","label":"Relationships"},{"id":"N8","label":"Divisional Charts"},{"id":"N9","label":"Dasha"},{"id":"N10","label":"Transit"},{"id":"N11","label":"Integrated Case Studies"},{"id":"N12","label":"Assessment"}],"edges":[["N1","N2"],["N2","N3"],["N3","N4"],["N4","N5"],["N5","N6"],["N6","N7"],["N7","N8"],["N8","N9"],["N9","N10"],["N10","N11"],["N11","N12"]],"chains":[["Foundation","Horoscope Structure","Core Components","Planetary Strength","Yogas","Ascendant Analysis","Relationships","Divisional Charts","Dasha","Transit","Integrated Case Studies","Assessment"]],"textVersion":"Foundation, then Horoscope Structure, then Core Components, then Planetary Strength, then Yogas, then Ascendant Analysis, then Relationships, then Divisional Charts, then Dasha, then Transit, then Integrated Case Studies, then Assessment."},
{"type":"para","text":"Read the diagram from top to bottom. An arrow means \"is needed before\". You do not have to hold the whole map in your head. It is here so that at any moment you know what you are doing and why."},
{"type":"table","caption":"Each stage, its modules and the question it answers","cols":4,"header":["Stage","Where it is taught","The question it answers","Start here"],"rows":[["1. Foundation","Module 1, Lessons 1.1 to 1.3","What is a chart, and what does it need to be built correctly?",[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.1","s":"module-01-lesson-01-what-is-a-kundali"}]],["2. Horoscope Structure","Module 1, Lessons 1.4 to 1.8","How is a chart laid out and drawn?",[{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.4","s":"module-01-lesson-04-lagna-rashis-bhavas-and-grahas"}]],["3. Core Components","Modules 2 to 4","What do the houses, the planets and the signs each stand for?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 2","s":"module-02-introduction"}]],["4. Planetary Strength","Modules 5 and 6","How well placed is a planet, and through what kind of house does it work?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 5","s":"module-05-introduction"}]],["5. Yogas","Modules 7 and 8","Which named combinations does a chart hold, and how firmly?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 7","s":"module-07-introduction"}]],["6. Ascendant Analysis","Module 9","How do houses, signs and lords combine for each of the twelve Lagnas?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 9","s":"module-09-introduction"}]],["7. Relationships","Module 10","How do the factors of a chart connect?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 10","s":"module-10-introduction"}]],["8. Divisional Charts","Module 11","What do the divisional charts add to the main chart?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 11","s":"module-11-introduction"}]],["9. Dasha","Module 12","Which planetary period is running?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 12","s":"module-12-introduction"}]],["10. Transit","Module 13","Where are the planets now, in relation to the birth chart?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 13","s":"module-13-introduction"}]],["11. Integrated Case Studies","Module 14","How do all the factors combine in one qualified reading?",[{"t":"a","x":"Module 14","s":"module-14-introduction"}]],["12. Assessment","Final assessment and practical examination","Can you now do it without help?",[{"t":"a","x":"Assessment Framework","s":"assessment-framework-and-completion-criteria"}]]]},
{"type":"callout","variant":"NOTE","lines":[{"k":"p","text":"Two stages share Module 1: Lessons 1.1 to 1.3 lay the foundation and Lessons 1.4 to 1.8 teach the structure. Module 6 sits in Planetary Strength although it classifies houses, because the group a house belongs to, such as an angular house or a challenging house, colours how a planet that owns or occupies it works."}]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"What Each Stage Adds"},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h3","text":"Stage 1: Foundation"},
{"type":"para","text":["This stage builds on nothing but the ordinary skills of the ",{"t":"a","x":"Prerequisites","s":"course-home-prerequisites"},". You learn what a ",{"t":"i","x":"Kundali"}," is, why ",{"t":"i","x":"Jyotisha"}," (Vedic astrology) uses the sidereal zodiac with the Lahiri ",{"t":"i","x":"Ayanamsha"},", and which birth facts a chart needs. By the end you can convert a clock time to Universal Time (UT), convert a tropical longitude to a sidereal one, grade a birth time as documented, recollected or rectified, and write the five settings on a chart."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h4","text":"Stage 2: Horoscope Structure"},
{"type":"para","text":["Stage 1 gave you reliable positions. This stage names the parts of a chart: the ",{"t":"i","x":"Lagna"}," (rising sign), the twelve ",{"t":"i","x":"Rashis"}," (signs), the twelve ",{"t":"i","x":"Bhavas"}," (houses), the nine ",{"t":"i","x":"Grahas"}," (planets) and the 27 ",{"t":"i","x":"Nakshatras"}," (lunar mansions). Then you learn the two drawing styles. By the end you can find the sign and lord of any house for any Lagna by counting, read a chart in the North or the South Indian style, and convert one to the other without moving a Graha. Nothing is interpreted yet. These are positions only."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h5","text":"Stage 3: Core Components"},
{"type":"para","text":"The structure gives you places and names. This stage teaches what each one stands for. Module 2 reads the houses as life domains, Module 3 reads the Grahas, and Module 4 reads the signs. By the end you can take one house, one Graha or one sign through the six steps and write a single hedged sentence. You also learn why none of the three is ever read alone."},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h6","text":"Stage 4: Planetary Strength"},
{"type":"para","text":["Stage 3 told you what a Graha, a house and a sign mean. Meaning is only part of the picture, because condition matters too. Module 5 teaches dignity (exaltation, debilitation, own sign and the rest), then combustion, retrogression, directional strength and a first look at ",{"t":"i","x":"Shadbala"}," (the classical six-fold strength). It also explains why dignity is not strength. Module 6 sorts the houses into groups such as ",{"t":"i","x":"Kendra"}," (angular), ",{"t":"i","x":"Trikona"}," (trine) and ",{"t":"i","x":"Dusthana"}," (challenging), because a Graha works through the houses it owns and occupies. By the end you can judge a planet's dignity at a given degree and name every group of a house."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h7","text":"Stage 5: Yogas"},
{"type":"para","text":["With meaning and strength in place, you can test combinations. Module 7 covers selected auspicious ",{"t":"i","x":"Yogas"},". Module 8 covers challenging Yogas and ",{"t":"i","x":"Doshas"},", without fear-based wording. By the end you can check a chart against a formation rule and give a verdict of present, verify or absent. You can also explain why a Yoga is a formation and not a promise, and why its strength and its effect in a life are separate questions."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h8","text":"Stage 6: Ascendant Analysis"},
{"type":"para","text":["Until now you found lords and houses one chart at a time. Module 9 puts them together for all twelve Ascendants. Because house ownership changes with the Lagna, the same Graha plays a different role in each. By the end you can list the lords, the functional roles and any ",{"t":"i","x":"Yogakaraka"}," for any Lagna, and write a hedged preliminary framework from House Theme, Sign Qualities, House Lord and Planetary Condition."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h9","text":"Stage 7: Relationships"},
{"type":"para","text":["Module 9 gave each planet and house a role for its Lagna. Factors in a chart do not act alone, though, and this stage studies their links. ",{"t":"i","x":"Sambandha"}," is the word for them, in four families: planet to planet, house to house, planet to house and planet to Nakshatra. A nine-point framework combines them. By the end you can trace the links of a chart and finish with what you can say and what you cannot yet say."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h10","text":"Stage 8: Divisional Charts"},
{"type":"para","text":["Stage 7 linked the factors of one chart. A divisional chart, or ",{"t":"i","x":"Varga"},", divides each sign again to look at one theme more closely, and it is always read beside the main Rashi chart (D1). Module 11 introduces sixteen Vargas and treats the ",{"t":"i","x":"Navamsha"}," (D9) and ",{"t":"i","x":"Dashamsha"}," (D10) in depth. By the end you can calculate a divisional sign, compare D1 with D9 in a qualified way, and explain why accurate birth time matters more for the finer divisions."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h11","text":"Stage 9: Dasha"},
{"type":"para","text":["Timing comes next. The ",{"t":"i","x":"Vimshottari Dasha"}," is a 120-year cycle of planetary periods that starts with the lord of the Moon's Nakshatra at birth. It builds on the Nakshatras of Stage 2, the lordship of Stage 6 and the dignity of Stage 4, and it asks you to check a Dasha lord in the Navamsha of Stage 8. By the end you can calculate the balance of the first ",{"t":"i","x":"Mahadasha"},", date the later periods, and assess a Dasha lord. You state the themes a period may bring into focus, and no event."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h12","text":"Stage 10: Transit"},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"i","x":"Gochara"}," is the present movement of the Grahas against the birth chart. A Dasha is a cycle calculated from the birth chart, whereas a transit is the sky as it stands on a given date. This stage builds on the natal chart (the birth chart), on counting houses from the Moon and from the Lagna, and on the Dasha. By the end you can count a transiting Graha's house from the Moon and the Lagna, check ",{"t":"i","x":"Sade Sati"}," for a stated date, and keep natal, Dasha and transit statements apart."]},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h13","text":"Stage 11: Integrated Case Studies"},
{"type":"para","text":"Every earlier stage now works together in a fifteen-step workflow, an evidence table and a five-part qualified reading. You practise on three public figures: Indira Gandhi, Mahatma Gandhi and Amitabh Bachchan. Their birth data are of unequal quality, so the data source and confidence are stated each time. Charts of real people are used only to practise method, and astrology is not claimed to explain or cause anyone's achievements. By the end you can complete the worksheet and write a reading that names no event, date or advice."},
{"type":"heading","level":3,"id":"h14","text":"Stage 12: Assessment"},
{"type":"para","text":"The last stage asks you to do it unaided. The Final Assessment covers the whole course. The Practical Chart-Reading Examination has three exercises of rising difficulty: identification, analysis and integration. The recommended pass mark for each is 60 marks out of 100, a setting your LMS administrator may adjust. The Course Completion page then sets out what you can and cannot do with what you have learned."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h15","text":"The Same Six Steps at Every Stage"},
{"type":"flow","dir":"LR","title":"The six-step method, repeated at every stage","nodes":[{"id":"N1","label":"Understand"},{"id":"N2","label":"Observe"},{"id":"N3","label":"Analyse"},{"id":"N4","label":"Establish Relationships"},{"id":"N5","label":"Verify"},{"id":"N6","label":"Interpret"},{"id":"N7","label":"Repeat at the next stage"}],"edges":[["N1","N2"],["N2","N3"],["N3","N4"],["N4","N5"],["N5","N6"],["N6","N7"]],"chains":[["Understand","Observe","Analyse","Establish Relationships","Verify","Interpret","Repeat at the next stage"]],"textVersion":"Understand, then Observe, then Analyse, then Establish Relationships, then Verify, then Interpret, then Repeat at the next stage."},
{"type":"para","text":"Every stage follows the same six steps. It is this course's own teaching sequence, not a classical procedure. What changes is how much each step contains."},
{"type":"table","caption":"How the six steps grow","cols":3,"header":["Step","In the early stages","In the later stages"],"rows":[["Understand","Learn what a term, house or Graha means","Recall what every factor, rule and Yoga means before you open a chart"],["Observe","Record signs, houses and degrees","Record Yogas, the running Dasha and the transits as well"],["Analyse","Take one factor at a time: its sign, house and lord","Add its dignity, its role for the Lagna and its house group"],["Establish Relationships","Count houses and see where each lord stands","Trace aspects, exchanges, Nakshatra links and divisional charts"],["Verify","Recount, and check the data and settings","Test each rule's conditions and look for contradicting indicators"],["Interpret","Write one hedged sentence","Write a qualified reading and say what stays uncertain"]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h16","text":"Milestone Checklist"},
{"type":"para","text":"Tick each milestone only when you can do it without the lesson open."},
{"type":"list","ordered":false,"checklist":true,"id":"ck1","items":[{"text":"Foundation: I can convert a birth time to UT and a tropical longitude to a sidereal one, and write the five chart settings.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.1"},{"text":"Horoscope Structure: I can draw one chart in both styles and name its Lagna, houses, signs and Grahas.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.2"},{"text":"Core Components: I can take a house, a Graha and a sign through the six steps, each to one hedged sentence.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.3"},{"text":"Planetary Strength: I can say what a planet's dignity does and does not show, and list every group of a house.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.4"},{"text":"Yogas: I can test a chart for a Yoga and give a verdict with the reason.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.5"},{"text":"Ascendant Analysis: I can list the lords, the functional roles and any Yogakaraka for each of the twelve Lagnas.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.6"},{"text":"Relationships: I can trace the links of a chart and write what I can and cannot yet say.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.7"},{"text":"Divisional Charts: I can find a divisional sign and compare D1 with D9 without reading D9 alone.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.8"},{"text":"Dasha: I can calculate the balance of the first Dasha and name the running Mahadasha and Antardasha.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.9"},{"text":"Transit: I can find the house of a transiting Graha from the Moon and the Lagna and keep natal, Dasha and transit statements apart.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.10"},{"text":"Integrated Case Studies: I can work through the fifteen steps and write a five-part qualified reading.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.11"},{"text":"Assessment: I completed the Final Assessment and the Practical Chart-Reading Examination and know my next step.","checked":false,"id":"ck1.12"}]},
{"type":"para","text":[{"t":"a","x":"How to Use This Course","s":"course-home-how-to-use"}," turns the map into a weekly routine, and the ",{"t":"a","x":"Syllabus","s":"course-home-syllabus"}," lists every module in detail."]}
]},
"course-home-conventions-and-assumptions":{"blocks":[
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h1","text":"Why This Page Exists"},
{"type":"para","text":["Every lesson makes choices: which zodiac, which house system, which spelling, which definition of a ",{"t":"i","x":"Yoga"},". This page states them in one place so that you can check any chart against them."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h2","text":"The Chart Framework"},
{"type":"table","caption":"The course defaults and the views that differ","cols":3,"header":["Setting","Course default","Other views, named in SCHOOLS boxes"],"rows":[["Approach","Parashari, after the traditional author of Brihat Parashara Hora Shastra","Jaimini, Tajika and Nadi are named only"],["Zodiac","Sidereal","Tropical (Western astrology)"],["Ayanamsha","Lahiri (Chitrapaksha)","Raman, Krishnamurti and others"],["Houses","Whole sign: the Lagna sign is the 1st house and the next sign the 2nd","Degree-based systems, such as Bhava Chalit, can place a planet in another house"],["Lordship","The seven classical planets; Rahu and Ketu own no signs","Some teachers give the nodes signs"],["Nodes","Mean node","True node, typically about 1 degree away and up to about 1.8"],["Node aspects","Rahu and Ketu aspect the 5th, 7th and 9th from their own position, like Jupiter (a course convention)","The 7th only, or none"],["Lakshmi Yoga","The 9th lord, with other conditions, in a Kendra or a Trikona","A Kendra only"],["Vipareeta Raja Yoga","The lord of the 6th, 8th or 12th in the 6th, 8th or 12th (Harsha, Sarala, Vimala)","Stricter readings"],["Taurus and Scorpio rising","Venus and Mars read as mixed Lagna lords (a course compromise)","Classical and modern tables differ"]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h3","text":"Notation"},
{"type":"table","caption":"Abbreviations and formats","cols":2,"header":["Item","Rule"],"rows":[["Codes",[{"t":"c","x":"Su Mo Ma Me Ju Ve Sa Ra Ke Asc"}," are used only in charts and compact tables, not in running prose, headings or quiz questions"]],["Position",["Degree sign and a plain apostrophe: ",{"t":"c","x":"Su 14°20'"}]],["Retrograde",[{"t":"c","x":"R"}," follows the degrees, as in ",{"t":"c","x":"Ve 20°05' R"}]],["No R","R is not written for Su, Mo, Ra, Ke or Asc. The Sun and Moon do not turn retrograde, and the mean node always moves backward"],["Signs, houses, Nakshatras","No abbreviations. Houses are the 1st to the 12th, and divisional charts are D1 to D60"],["Time and date","24-hour clock, UT, UTC+05:30, and dates such as 26 October 2026"],["Dasha periods","Saturn/Mercury is a Saturn Mahadasha with a Mercury Antardasha"]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h4","text":"Spelling"},
{"type":"para","text":"Sanskrit is written in plain English letters, with no accents and one spelling for each term. Long vowels are not marked (Kanya, Tula). The letters sh, ch and ri stand for the sounds in Shani, Chandra and Vrishabha, and a final a is kept (Dasha, Gochara). A few follow the course specification or established English use, such as Meena and Vrischika."},
{"type":"table","caption":"Course spellings and common alternatives","cols":2,"header":["Course spelling","Also seen"],"rows":[["Vrischika","Vrishchika"],["Meena","Mina, Meen"],["Shani","Sani"],["Dasha","Dasa"],["Gochara","Gochar"],["Moolatrikona","Mulatrikona"],["Yogakaraka","Yoga Karaka"],["Mangal Dosha","Manglik Dosha, Kuja Dosha"],["Sade Sati","Sadhe Sati"]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h5","text":"How SCHOOLS Boxes Work"},
{"type":"para","text":["Vedic astrology (",{"t":"i","x":"Jyotisha"},") has no single rulebook. Where texts, teachers or software differ, a SCHOOLS box (labelled School Variation on screen and SCHOOLS DIFFER in print) gives the course default first, then names the variants and who holds them, as far as the sources show. A point that could not be confirmed is said to be unconfirmed. A classical principle, a commonly used convention and an illustrative example are kept apart, and a modern rule is not credited to a classical text."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h6","text":"Hypothetical Charts and Real People"},
{"type":"para","text":["A hypothetical chart is constructed for teaching and is not a real person; its title says so. It is built to be consistent (Mercury within 28 degrees of the Sun, Venus within 47, Rahu exactly opposite Ketu), and every Yoga or ",{"t":"i","x":"Dosha"}," stated about it has been checked against the course rules. Sample Horoscope A of ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 1.6","s":"module-01-lesson-06-reading-the-north-indian-chart"}," is the first and returns in later modules."]},
{"type":"para","text":["Data about real people appear only in the three case studies of ",{"t":"a","x":"Module 14","s":"module-14-introduction"},". Each states its sources and a confidence label: DOCUMENTED, REPORTED, NOT DOCUMENTED, or DISPUTED or UNVERIFIED, for birth facts, and FIRM or PROVISIONAL for chart features that depend on the minute of birth. A label grades the data, not the person. The transit examples of Module 13 set hypothetical charts against the real sky of a stated date."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h7","text":"The Order of Reading a Chart"},
{"type":"para","text":["The course reads a chart in this order. Check the data and settings. Find the ",{"t":"i","x":"Lagna"}," and the structure. Examine the Lagna lord, the houses, their signs and their lords. Assess dignity. Trace the relationships. Test Yogas. Compare divisional charts with D1. Find the ",{"t":"i","x":"Dasha"},", then the transits. Weigh supporting and contradicting indicators, and state a qualified reading with its limits. ",{"t":"a","x":"Lesson 14.1","s":"module-14-lesson-01-the-fifteen-step-chart-analysis-workflow"}," gives the fifteen steps in full."]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h8","text":"Prediction and Determinism"},
{"type":"para","text":"The course teaches no event predictions. A chart is read as an account of themes and tendencies in the tradition's terms, not as fixed destiny. Choice, effort and circumstances matter, and a chart is one lens on a life. A Yoga is a formation, not a promise, and a Dasha or a transit may bring themes into focus but fixes no event. The wording follows: indicates, suggests, tends to, traditionally associated with."},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h9","text":"Seven Standard Statements"},
{"type":"para","text":"These sentences recur, sometimes reworded."},
{"type":"table","caption":"The seven standard statements","cols":3,"header":["ID","Use","Statement"],"rows":[["S1","Framework","This course teaches Vedic astrology (Jyotisha) as a traditional interpretive framework, not as a scientifically established method."],["S2","No promises","No chart reading in this course is a prediction or a guarantee; every interpretation is a hypothesis to examine."],["S3","No advice","Nothing in this course is medical, legal or financial advice; body associations are traditional symbolism."],["S4","Remedies","Remedies, where mentioned, are tradition-specific practices and are not guaranteed solutions."],["S5","Real people","Real-person charts are used only to practise method, with source and confidence stated; astrology is not claimed to explain or cause anyone's achievements."],["S6","Hypothetical charts","Hypothetical charts are constructed for teaching and are not real people."],["S7","Schools","Where traditions differ, this course states its default and names the variants."]]},
{"type":"heading","level":2,"id":"h10","text":"The Instructional Assumptions"},
{"type":"para","text":"The course rests on 27 assumptions. They are numbered 1 to 27 here."},
{"type":"list","ordered":true,"items":[{"text":"You are a complete beginner who can read a simple table and do basic arithmetic.","ordered":true,"n":1},{"text":"The suggested pace is about 100 hours over 16 weeks, or 6 to 7 hours a week. The final assessment, practical examination and workbook fit inside that figure. It is a suggestion.","ordered":true,"n":2},{"text":"The chart framework is the one in the table above.","ordered":true,"n":3},{"text":"Each default is a teaching choice. SCHOOLS boxes name variants without implying that one method is the only correct one.","ordered":true,"n":4},{"text":"Astrology is taught as a traditional interpretive framework, not as scientifically established, and no predictive accuracy is claimed.","ordered":true,"n":5},{"text":"Every interpretation is a hypothesis to examine.","ordered":true,"n":6},{"text":"The course gives no medical, legal or financial advice, and body associations are traditional symbolism.","ordered":true,"n":7},{"text":"Remedies, where described, are tradition-specific practices without guaranteed results, and the course does not prescribe them.","ordered":true,"n":8},{"text":"Hypothetical charts are labelled. Real-person data appear only in the case studies, with sources and confidence labels.","ordered":true,"n":9},{"text":"Every number in a worked example comes from a stated tool or table. If you use a free chart tool, check its settings. The course endorses no product.","ordered":true,"n":10},{"text":"Examples assume an accurate birth time unless stated. Time-dependent features are provisional when it is uncertain.","ordered":true,"n":11},{"text":["Sanskrit is introduced gradually, term first and English second, with one plain spelling per term. Signs and planets are the exception: English first, with the Sanskrit name beside it at first use, as in Aries (",{"t":"i","x":"Mesha"},")."],"ordered":true,"n":12},{"text":"Classical books are named as general sources, with no verses and no chapter or verse numbers. The Reference Library points to the exact texts.","ordered":true,"n":13},{"text":"The 14 modules are cumulative. Later modules assume the earlier ones.","ordered":true,"n":14},{"text":"Pass marks and completion criteria are recommended settings that the LMS administrator may adjust.","ordered":true,"n":15},{"text":"The course is foundational and does not make you an expert predictive astrologer.","ordered":true,"n":16},{"text":["Examples use concrete signs, houses and degrees. Transit examples state the reference date, zodiac and ",{"t":"i","x":"Ayanamsha"},"."],"ordered":true,"n":17},{"text":"This is the first edition, October 2026. Dated facts such as the Ayanamsha value, transit positions and software menus will age.","ordered":true,"n":18},{"text":"The course uses British English spelling.","ordered":true,"n":19},{"text":["The names of the course team, the lineage and the brand are spelled as the course owner supplied them, and the profile in ",{"t":"a","x":"About the Instructors and Course Team","s":"course-home-instructor"}," uses only supplied facts."],"ordered":true,"n":20},{"text":"Interactive activities have a text alternative. No meaning depends on colour alone. Tables have at most six columns, and no lesson needs an external image.","ordered":true,"n":21},{"text":"Another person's birth data is used only with their permission. The course examples are hypothetical, apart from the three public-figure case studies of Module 14, which use data already published and state their sources.","ordered":true,"n":22},{"text":"Material on challenging combinations informs and does not alarm. If one worries you in your own chart, remember that it is one factor in a framework, and seek qualified support.","ordered":true,"n":23},{"text":["Horoscope matching (",{"t":"i","x":"Kuta"},"), ",{"t":"i","x":"Muhurta"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Prashna"},", ",{"t":"i","x":"Ashtakavarga"},", the Jaimini, Tajika and Nadi systems and other Dasha systems are named only and are not taught."],"ordered":true,"n":24},{"text":"The six-step sequence is this course's own teaching sequence, not a classical procedure.","ordered":true,"n":25},{"text":"Kala Sarpa Yoga, the four aim groups of houses (Dharma, Artha, Kama, Moksha) and the 2nd-5th-9th-11th lord-link rule for Dhana Yoga are modern conventions. Kendradhipati Dosha, Trishadaya and the name Dhana Yoga are classical terms that the course respells or reuses. It marks each.","ordered":true,"n":26},{"text":"The course is self-paced with no live class assumed. Answers and explanations are in the Answer Keys.","ordered":true,"n":27}]}
]}
}};
